WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:24.690
JOSHUA COHEN: I think
we can get started.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.470
I'd like to welcome you all
to this last session of this

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.347
two-day colloquium--

00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:32.180
just to make sure you're
in the right room--

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:39.140
this is the 50th anniversary
celebration session for.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.750
And our question for
this final session

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.820
is, is capitalism
good for democracy?

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.640
Or we could put the question
more colorlessly as,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.940
what is the relationship between
capitalism and democracy?

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.005
Now I have the--

00:00:55.005 --> 00:00:57.380
I guess there's been enough
turnover between the two days

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.460
that I should
reintroduce myself.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.640
My Josh Cohen, I
teach in philosophy,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.600
and I'm the head of the
Political Science Department.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.170
And for this session, I've
been given the easy task

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:14.030
of introducing a question
that requires no explanation

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.830
and speakers that require no
introduction, so I'll be brief.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.820
At least-- well, it may not
seem like I'm being brief,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.406
but it'll be brief for me.

00:01:24.406 --> 00:01:27.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.070
So writing in the middle
of the 19th century,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.890
Marx proposed some
answers to the questions

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.590
of today's session.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.940
He said, "capitalist democracy
is possible only as,"

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:46.010
in his words, "a spasmodic
exceptional state of things.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.790
It's impossible," he
continues, "as the normal form

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.390
of society."

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:55.350
And the reasoning for this
conclusion was straightforward.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.790
He said, "the classes whose
social slavery the constitution

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:03.020
is to perpetuate--" proletariat,
peasantry, petty bourgeoisie--

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.930
"it--" that is, democracy--

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.970
"puts in possession
of political power

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.130
through universal suffrage.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.710
And from the class
whose social power it

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.040
sanctions--" the bourgeoisie--

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.860
"it withdraws the political
guarantees of this power.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.840
It forces the
political rule of the

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.170
bourgeoisie into
democratic conditions which

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:27.890
at every moment help the
hostile classes to victory

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.950
and jeopardize the very
foundations of bourgeois

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.390
society.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.690
From the ones, it demands that
they should not go forward

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.440
from political to social
emancipation; from the others,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.020
that they should not
go back from social

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.440
to political restoration."

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.030
Now Marx was hardly
alone in thinking

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.120
that the political
equality ingredient

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:56.770
in a democratic state couldn't
coexist with the inequality

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.220
and private power ingredient
in a capitalist economy,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:04.120
that either democracy or
capitalism would win out.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.970
In the mid-19th century,
he shared that outlook

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.840
with conservative opponents
of universal suffrage

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.340
who feared the very thing
that Marx hoped for.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.600
When this school was
founded in 1950 at MIT,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.810
it wasn't uncommon to
think that the experience

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.270
of the previous 30 years--

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:29.410
Depression, fascism,
communism, and world war--

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.600
had demonstrated the
truth of these earlier

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.900
argument about the
irreconcilability of capitalism

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.670
and democracy.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.860
To be sure, there
were some who thought

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.240
that in the United States, at
least, the New Deal had saved

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.970
democratic capitalism,
but it's worth

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.270
recalling that conservative
opponents of the New Deal

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:53.260
thought that had paved
the way to a new serfdom,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.930
and that even its
proponents, the New

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.850
Deal and democratic
capitalism, may not

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:02.720
have been entirely confident
of its long-term stability.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.310
Now something has changed
in our understanding

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.330
of the combination of
capitalism and democracy.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.700
Marx, as well as the
conservative critics

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.400
of democracy, may have been
onto something and thinking

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.920
that there are interesting
tensions between capitalism

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.329
and democracy.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.120
Maybe it's an-- maybe
it's an awkward marriage,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.530
but not every awkward
union ends badly.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.140
In any case, the
confident thesis

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.690
that capitalist
democracy couldn't last

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.850
has been overtaken by events.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.840
And the aim of our
session this afternoon

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.640
is to explore some
of the changes

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.390
in the world and changes in
thinking about the world that

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.090
have taken place over
the past 50 years

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.430
and led to different
conceptions of the relationship

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.440
between capitalism
and democracy.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.000
We have three very
distinguished speakers with us

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.580
who'll each speak for
about 15 to 20 minutes

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.330
on our topic, after which we'll
have some open discussion.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.700
And I'll introduce
them now in the order

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.470
in which they'll speak.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.080
Our first speaker
is Robert Solow,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.540
who is Institute Professor
Emeritus and Professor

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.480
of Economics Emeritus
at MIT, winner

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.360
of a Nobel Prize in
economics and the National

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.670
Medal of Science.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:20.500
Bob is publicly
known for his work

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.270
on technological change
and economic growth,

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:26.550
but he's secretly a Luddite
who doesn't use email.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.665
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:29.665 --> 00:05:31.040
I shouldn't say
doesn't, I should

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.170
say refuses to use email
as an act of refusal

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.670
as I recently rediscovered.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:41.051
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:45.380
Kenneth Arrow is the Joan Kenney
Professor of Economics Emeritus

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.970
at Stanford.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:51.290
Also winner of a Nobel Prize in
economics and a National Medal

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.400
of Science.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.730
And there are three
bodies of work, at least,

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:59.270
that are closely associated
with his name, one of which

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.270
precisely specifies the
conditions under which Adam

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.390
Smith's invisible hand
hypothesis about markets

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:09.200
is true, a second
describes the problems

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.720
in combining the separate
values of individuals

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.840
into a coherent
social valuation,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.480
and a third on issues
about decision-making

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.290
under uncertainty.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:22.420
Suzanne Berger,
our third speaker,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.880
is the Rafael Doorman
and Helen Staubach

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.310
Professor of Political
Science and Director

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.170
of MIT's MISTI program--

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:34.090
that is the MIT Initiative
on Science and Technology.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.600
She's written on French
politics, and more broadly,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.260
on the disparate economic impact
of different national ways

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:43.780
of organizing political life.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:47.980
Suzanne, I can also testify
from after 24 years,

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.820
is the best colleague
you could imagine having.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:56.530
Now I know Bob Solow
and Suzanne Berger well,

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:59.860
although I should say that Ken
Arrow is the only one I ever

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.170
took courses from-- he doesn't
know about-- when I was

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.330
a graduate student at Harvard.

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:09.550
I twice in consecutive
years sat through his course

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.100
on micro theory.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.077
I can't say that I--

00:07:14.077 --> 00:07:17.416
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.165
--why I sat through it twice.

00:07:21.165 --> 00:07:23.570
But I did.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.230
But I know-- I do know all
three of-- the work of all three

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.313
of them well, and I do
want to make a comment that

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:31.600
applies to each, which is
that they all are known

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.680
for addressing topics
of large significance

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.670
with attention to the demands
of reason and evidence,

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:42.790
but also without ever
losing sight of the fact

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:46.270
that the social sciences
are moral sciences

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:51.126
in which the final
stakes are human lives.

00:07:51.126 --> 00:07:52.114
Bob.

00:07:52.114 --> 00:07:55.572
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.360
ROBERT SOLOW: Is it OK--

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.750
can I be heard if I
just sit here and--

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.050
it's on.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:04.740
OK.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.350
JOSHUA COHEN: Is it OK
with the people up there?

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:06.920
OK.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:07.820
Good.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.740
I don't mean you in the back,
I mean them in the booth.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.170
It's OK with them.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.300
OK, good.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:18.500
ROBERT SOLOW: Well, there
was a famous recent occasion

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.570
in the history of this
country when a question asked

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:27.110
of an important person
was answered by the reply,

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.210
it depends on what
you mean by "is."

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:37.280
The question we're supposed to
discuss here is capitalism--

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.570
is democracy-- which is it?

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:44.310
Is capitalism good
for democracy?

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.210
But in this case, I think
it depends on what you

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:50.360
mean by capitalism, actually.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:57.110
I should start by telling
you in an old cliche

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.720
where I come from,
where I'm coming from.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:06.470
My most deeply-held political
belief is that power corrupts.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:10.400
I don't know if absolute
power corrupts absolutely,

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.590
these limiting propositions
are sometimes hard to work out,

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:18.440
but I do think as I look
at the political world

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.300
that power corrupts.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:27.800
And therefore, for me, the
basic problem of democracy

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.750
is to avoid undue
concentrations of power,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:38.750
to avoid the accumulation
of too much power

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:44.010
almost anywhere, actually, and
certainly in the wrong places.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:47.750
The problem is delicate because
some concentrations of power

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:52.220
are needed in a society because
there are economies of scale--

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:54.780
not only economies of
scale in production,

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.080
but economies of scale
in the performance

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:01.910
of other social functions,
and it's very hard

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:07.970
to get them done well
without some group body

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:14.300
institution acquiring a
certain amount of power.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.580
It seems to me, and
it's always seemed to me

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:21.590
since I was a kid, actually,
that I hated school.

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:27.690
That's probably where I got
this notion that power corrupts.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:31.140
But this goes back
a long way with me.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.880
It always seemed
to me that this is

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.780
the really fundamental
argument against socialism

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.910
as an alternative to capitalism.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:46.500
It's true that there
is no real example

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:51.630
of the successful running
of a socialist economy.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.810
But even if that
could be fixed, even

00:10:54.810 --> 00:11:00.780
if one could find ways of
operating a socialist economy

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:07.020
efficiently, the
fact that it means,

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:10.080
it involves giving
economic power

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.150
to the state in a big way--

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:14.520
that is, attaching
economic power

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:18.540
to the locus of political
power strikes me

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.470
as a recipe for tyranny.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.310
To have, from a certain
degree of abstraction,

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:32.700
only one employer in a
society, one source of finance

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:41.280
and all that suggests to me
that corruption will result.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:47.310
In my youth, I had friends
who thought that Stalinism

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:52.140
was a perversion of
socialism, and I wondered even

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:56.160
then if they were not wrong.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.520
I personally would
have little or nothing

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.910
to say against
democratic socialism,

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:08.490
except the suspicion
that it's an oxymoron.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.280
All the socialism as
we have observed--

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.680
not so many, but all
the ones we've observed

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.530
seem to have either originated
or finished in tyranny.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:25.410
One of them seems just to
have finished the other day.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:31.490
So coming now to the
precise question posed

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:38.120
for this meeting,
capitalism has going for it

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:43.190
that on paper, at least,
it separates economic power

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:48.860
from political power, and this
was thought about explicitly

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:53.480
in the philosophical
origins of people

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:54.890
like Locke I'm thinking of.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:03.440
On paper, capitalism
turns private property

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.500
into a buffer for the individual
against a political power.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:16.180
It defines a sphere in
which you are your own--

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:20.360
in which you are your own boss.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:26.820
A person's home is his or her
castle to put it that way.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.090
But in fact, it's
not that simple.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:32.390
It's very far from
being that simple.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:36.480
We know from observation-- and
there are some good reasons

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.190
in principle, in theory
for believing it--

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:43.940
that laissez faire
capitalism tends

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:49.910
to generate vast inequalities
of income and even vaster

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.940
inequalities of wealth.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:58.280
Any capitalist society living
its natural life, so to speak,

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:02.630
will make these big extremes,
and in particular, there

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:09.500
will be a group of very rich
and very wealthy people.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:14.300
Leaving aside whether
that kind of inequality

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:18.230
is intrinsically a good
thing or a bad thing,

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:22.610
it seems to me that there
is an endemic tendency

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:28.070
in capitalist democracies for
the very wealthy to want to buy

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:30.530
political power,
to use their wealth

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.860
to purchase political power.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:38.000
We see that all
around us here today,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.270
most clearly in
campaign financing

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:47.600
where the influence of money
on the electoral process

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:52.920
is, I think, a real
danger for democracy.

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.500
And this is not
true only of the US.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:01.270
Helmut Kohl got himself
into a lot of trouble

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:04.490
for what, by American
standards, would

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:08.840
be a mere peccadillo, would
be something you would barely

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:13.810
report to your accountant.

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:20.840
And the same thing is true in
other democratic capitalist

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:27.140
countries, there is a tendency
for wealth to want to buy--

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.370
to want to buy power.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:32.990
It's not-- Texas oil tycoons
do not play an important role

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:36.170
in our political life because
of their intellectual or moral

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:39.710
quality, particularly.

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:41.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:45.750
During the Reagan and
Bush administrations,

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.690
we had the spectacle-- and I--

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:51.380
I guess still naive enough
to have been shocked by it,

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:54.320
not so many other people were.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:57.740
We had the spectacle
of the representatives

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:03.470
of big business sitting with
the Republican majorities

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:07.760
of congressional committees
writing up and marking up

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.730
legislation.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:18.110
So the trouble with
unregulated capitalism

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:25.490
is that political power gets
absorbed by economic power

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.070
to a larger or lesser
extent, but sometimes

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:30.180
to a very large extent.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.220
And so we run the
danger of ending up

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:39.410
from this point of view
where socialism ends up,

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.860
only by the back door,
but with economic power

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:49.040
and political power
sitting in the same hands.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:54.800
And this suggests to me
that probably the best

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:03.490
we can do for democracy is
partially regulated capitalism.

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.740
And I have-- one
could go on at length

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.920
about the kinds of
regulation that are required,

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:14.250
but I'll just
mention two or three.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:18.990
First of all,
capitalism which is--

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:21.089
does engage-- in which
the government, at least,

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:24.510
engages in redistribution
of income and wealth

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:31.320
to relieve inequality and to
limit the extreme accumulation

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:39.790
of wealth and therefore of both
economic and political power.

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:43.710
Secondly, one wants
democratic capitalism

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:47.700
in which there is serious
public intervention

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:51.310
to limit monopoly
and exploitation,

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:54.330
and to preserve competition--

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:58.260
real competition wherever
that is possible.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:03.360
And third, the third thing
I want to mention now

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:09.420
is the public provision of
seriously good education

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:14.200
for everybody for the
purpose not only of providing

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:18.360
a skilled labor force
and all that stuff,

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:22.100
but to promote economic
and social mobility,

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:26.340
to promote the
circulation of people

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.720
through the economic system.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.930
One could imagine
easily, because we

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:40.350
do other forms of
intervention in economic life,

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.390
but it's the--

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:47.370
it seems to me the natural
habitat for democracy

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:53.400
is not unregulated capitalism,
but regulated capitalism,

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:56.820
what we used to call
the mixed economy.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:01.530
There will surely be imperfect,
but it's the best we can do.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:06.030
I want to make only
one more remark now.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:10.080
Some of you will have noticed
that the sort of society

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:16.110
I'm suggesting as a good idea
is exactly what Hayek, Friedrich

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:21.660
Hayek in The Road to
Serfdom was arguing against.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:26.040
Josh mentioned The Road
to Serfdom parenthetically

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:29.580
in his introductory remarks.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.700
Hayek thought, I
presume seriously,

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:36.960
that the first steps toward
serfdom, what I was earlier

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:40.170
calling tyranny, are
precisely the steps

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:45.450
that I think are necessary
to preserve the compatibility

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:49.620
of capitalism and democracy.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:54.360
As a forecast, of course,
The Road to Serfdom

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:59.190
stands as one of the
really great failures,

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:03.390
like Marx's forecast of the
progressive impoverishment

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:08.040
of the working class in
capitalist economies.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.040
It's 50 or more years
since The Road to Serfdom,

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:16.830
and during that time,
the slippery slope

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:23.280
from antitrust
policy or regulation

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:31.260
of other kinds toward serfdom
has not shown itself at all.

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:32.850
I think Hayek could
have been seen

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:37.200
to be wrong on the day
The Road to Serfdom

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.970
was published because
it was perfectly

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:48.060
visible that those capitalist
countries like Norway,

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:51.570
the Netherlands,
Sweden, which did more

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:57.360
interfering in the economy
and ought, according to Hayek,

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.930
have been further down the
road to serfdom than the more

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:04.680
laissez faire places
like the United States,

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.970
we're not like that at all.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:10.020
They were both more
regulated than the US,

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:17.010
and generally more free as
more open to unconventional

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:21.570
ideas than American society was.

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:25.320
That may still be true
comparing those countries.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:27.540
There is no reason
to suppose that there

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:31.140
is a slippery slope
from, again, what used

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:36.780
to be called the middle
way toward Hayek's

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:40.980
notion of serfdom, and
I guess the middle way--

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:44.250
not necessarily
Sweden-- is what I've

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.600
been advocating this afternoon.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:48.090
Thank you.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:08.560
KENNETH ARROW: First of
all, I should explain--

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:12.900
it may be quite obvious to
you that I'm not a historical

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.190
scholar or a
political scientist--

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:22.290
perhaps we'll hear from the
professional after I speak--

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:26.743
that I'm not-- this is
not research of mine

00:22:26.743 --> 00:22:27.785
that I'm acquainted with.

00:22:27.785 --> 00:22:29.880
And it's possible
to argue, however,

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:31.870
this is a subject
in which researchers

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:36.360
in the real scientific sense
is unlikely to cast much

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:40.290
light as a general impression--
what we know generally make up

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.160
most, at least, of
what there is to say,

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:44.760
though no doubt it would
be illumination in studying

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:48.230
particular historical incidents
in the, say, for example,

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:50.820
of changes from
democracy to the opposite

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:57.170
or dictatorships of one kind
or another or vice versa.

00:22:57.170 --> 00:23:00.240
I also-- in fact, I
must say, having been--

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:05.370
I would have given a simple
answer to this question

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:06.390
before I was asked--

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:08.610
in fact, I did on
one occasion, and I

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:10.500
participated in a
written symposium

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.975
on the 25th or
30th anniversary--

00:23:12.975 --> 00:23:17.510
I forgot now-- of Joseph
Schumpeter's Capitalism,

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:22.220
Socialism, and
Democracy in which

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.840
he said capitalism seems
to be a necessary condition

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:26.730
for democracy,
though clearly not

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.380
a sufficient one
since there are plenty

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.660
of capitalist dictatorships.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.440
And there have been--

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:36.960
there have been and even--
and there are today.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:40.140
But having this
question posed, I've

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:43.410
been thinking a lot
about it, and so

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:47.070
really I don't know
whether I should continue,

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.170
but anything I
say now is sort of

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:52.840
in the middle of
development of my thinking.

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:54.270
One of the interesting
questions--

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:55.800
well, you can ask
in the first place

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:57.570
what the terms mean--
what is democracy

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.100
and what is capitalism?

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.720
Democracy, I think--

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:01.680
I don't think we're--

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.805
the working day definitions
are good enough for us.

00:24:03.805 --> 00:24:08.400
I mean, some nominal legal
equality of political power

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:10.710
along a very broad
class of people.

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:16.540
And with--
accompanied, of course,

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:18.370
by the political freedom.

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:20.490
The freedom of the
press, freedom of speech,

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.170
and freedom of
assembly and so forth

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.087
which are necessary to
implement-- the freedom

00:24:24.087 --> 00:24:25.920
to criticize, some kind
of-- and of course,

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.465
you can get then into very
interesting questions--

00:24:28.465 --> 00:24:31.583
for example, access to
government documents

00:24:31.583 --> 00:24:32.250
becomes a thing.

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:33.665
There's a difference, for
example, between Great Britain

00:24:33.665 --> 00:24:35.248
and the United States
in that respect.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:39.913
And there are other differences.

00:24:39.913 --> 00:24:42.080
What I found a little more
puzzling, and I must say,

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:43.660
I don't even know the answer.

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:46.710
I'm not prepared to give an
answer-- is what is capitalism?

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:48.930
The reason that's important
is because in a sense,

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:51.097
in the modern day world,
we think of the alternative

00:24:51.097 --> 00:24:54.330
is capitalism or socialism.

00:24:54.330 --> 00:25:01.080
Historically-- of course, there
was a pre-capitalist feudal--

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.550
at least in the Western Europe--

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:05.340
feudal-- feudalism
as an alternative.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.110
And the way we usually
tell the history--

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.690
I don't know-- I don't
know how accurate it is--

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:12.390
of the emergence of the
more democratic regimes,

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:15.960
say the gradual developments
in Great Britain or the more

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:20.205
sudden developments,
shall I say, in France--

00:25:20.205 --> 00:25:23.070
revolutionary in the
strict sense-- the--

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:26.850
are-- the idea is usually
it's the middle classes,

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:29.580
but the emerge-- there was
an emerging capitalist system

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.060
within a feudal
framework, and somehow,

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:35.780
along good Marxist lines,
there was a-- these two were

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.830
irreconcilable in a democracy.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:39.870
It was a shift of
political power

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:43.470
away from the king and
nobles and all that.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:46.560
I'm sure that any real historian
would tell me this is all--

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:49.915
it's not really right,
and of course, we have--

00:25:49.915 --> 00:25:51.540
although it does
correspond, I believe,

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.660
to [INAUDIBLE] account,
a rather famous account

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:57.300
of the development the French
Revolution, it was pointed out

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.918
it was the more progressive
regions of France

00:25:59.918 --> 00:26:00.960
where the revolution was.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:03.660
It was not the hopelessly
impoverished regions

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:06.300
where the peasants were
totally repressed that

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.250
was the center of
revolutionary activity.

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:10.965
So you can give that more
than one interpretation.

00:26:10.965 --> 00:26:12.090
One is that the bourgeois--

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:13.890
So there is this
association, then,

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:16.770
of the emergence of
democracy with capitalism.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:19.260
Of course, you go into little
details, some of this fuss

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:19.820
gets lost.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:23.770
Then why did Germany develop in
a very different way of 1914,

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.360
Germany was a limited democracy.

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:28.320
That said, there was a popular
election, universal suffrage,

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:32.490
but the powers of the Reichstag
were just very much less

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:34.200
than those of the
British parliament.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:38.100
So the emperor and
his cabinet still,

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.410
although they had to
deal with the Reichstag,

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:42.480
were by no means
subservient to it

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:44.610
in the way that the
British cabinet is

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:47.400
or the American
president being elected

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:51.880
or these different
forms of responsibility.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:53.400
So the capital-- so they--

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:56.100
now one way of looking at it
is maybe the capitalist systems

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:57.030
were different.

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:59.670
I'll come back to this
in several forms, but I--

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:02.210
the truth is I've no
very definite answer

00:27:02.210 --> 00:27:03.090
as to what it is.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:05.090
And this is a matter and
suddenly a great moment

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.280
not merely for the
analysis of democracy,

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:09.260
but even for the analysis
of economic growth,

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:12.620
as we'll come back
to at a later point.

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:15.120
The plus side, of course, why
capitalism might be thought of

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:21.350
to be a great contributor to
the preservation of democracy

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:26.060
is the argument that
Bob has already given,

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:28.850
namely that it avoids
concentration of power.

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:31.190
At least it spreads power--
economic power is different

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:34.460
from political power, it can
provide a kind of a shield

00:27:34.460 --> 00:27:36.530
for the--

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:37.850
for dissidents.

00:27:37.850 --> 00:27:40.108
For dissidents, it gives
a kind of protection.

00:27:42.283 --> 00:27:44.450
The more I thought about
it, the connection to this,

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:47.610
the more-- the less convincing
this argument came to me.

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.580
And there's nothing
wrong with it in itself,

00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:52.310
but the trouble is, if you look
at the way political power is--

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:56.240
anti-democratic political
power is exercised,

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.420
or even democratic political
power and its populists

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:03.500
and majoritarian phases
when it becomes--

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.760
when the majority
oppresses the minority,

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:09.560
as has certainly happened
in various episodes,

00:28:09.560 --> 00:28:12.405
but certainly if we
consider a dictatorship,

00:28:12.405 --> 00:28:14.030
the economic power
the state is usually

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:16.440
one of the least
powerful weapons.

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:20.810
I mean, killing people,
power, putting them in prison,

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:22.403
torturing them--

00:28:22.403 --> 00:28:23.820
rather considerably
more effective

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:25.888
than depriving them of jobs.

00:28:25.888 --> 00:28:27.680
I think you'll find it
in the Soviet Union,

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.280
for example, dissidents who were
not-- who, say, were allowed

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:32.790
out of prison.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:34.400
They [INAUDIBLE] They got jobs.

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:35.450
They weren't kept out.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:37.970
They might not get the jobs
they felt they deserved,

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:40.490
but even these dissident
intellectuals usually

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.725
worked as a translator,
some kind of motive,

00:28:43.725 --> 00:28:45.933
and they weren't
really starved to death

00:28:45.933 --> 00:28:47.100
except in a couple of cases.

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:48.892
Well, they wanted
to go after them.

00:28:48.892 --> 00:28:51.350
They went after them in much
more brutal and effective ways

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:52.880
than economics.

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:55.760
So the economic power is
instrument of repression.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:57.380
It strikes me as
kind of marginal.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.240
The additional
economic power conveyed

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:05.280
by the gulag, the concentration
camps for dissidents in Germany

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:06.170
is a--

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:07.820
not thinking about
death camps now,

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:10.890
but the political
concentration camps.

00:29:10.890 --> 00:29:12.920
These are the ways--

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:14.660
well, or to take
another example,

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:17.920
the beginning of the Chinese--

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:20.210
when the Chinese
communists took over,

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:24.523
somehow about 30 million former
landlords have disappeared.

00:29:24.523 --> 00:29:26.940
And I think, yeah, now it's
pretty clear, they were just--

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:29.490
I mean, the statement was
they were being deported

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.650
to the border provinces,
but it's pretty clear now,

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:32.880
they were all just killed.

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:33.963
They didn't want anything.

00:29:33.963 --> 00:29:37.350
So this was not wasting time
just taking away their land,

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:42.670
for example, which might be
an example of economic power.

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:44.160
It wasn't even--
I mean, if China--

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:45.935
like all of these
regimes, you'll

00:29:45.935 --> 00:29:47.310
always find
exceptions people who

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:52.733
were treated perfectly well,
old capitalists and landlords

00:29:52.733 --> 00:29:54.150
would be perfectly
well, but there

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:57.750
was a fair amount of just
plain prison and worse.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:04.650
Also, if capitalism
is good for democracy,

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:08.450
one might expect that
more capitalism--

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:11.190
I'm saying there may be
degrees of capitalism--

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:14.410
should lead to more democracy.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:16.410
Well, of course there's
a lot of problems-- what

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.920
do I mean by more capitalism?

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:19.920
Well, one test is the
size of the state.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:23.850
For example, what percentage
of the national income

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:26.250
of a country goes
through the state?

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:29.520
Roughly, the tax bite.

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:31.690
And as Bob has
already pointed out,

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:34.930
there are countries like
Sweden, which to this day,

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:36.360
over 50% of the--

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:40.410
50% or so of the national
income goes through the state.

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:43.500
And nobody would allege that
they're less democratic--

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:45.390
in fact, some people
say the opposite--

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.320
than those with much smaller,
let's say, like Japan

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:51.368
and United States, which have
considerably smaller fractions

00:30:51.368 --> 00:30:52.160
going through them.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:56.130
And most European countries
are between Sweden on one hand

00:30:56.130 --> 00:30:58.495
and the United
States on the other

00:30:58.495 --> 00:31:00.870
So it doesn't appear the size
of-- at least empirically--

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:02.740
this is a very crude
empirical statement,

00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:06.060
but at least on a contemporary--
using cross-section analysis,

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:08.953
as we would say, you don't
see much evidence of this.

00:31:08.953 --> 00:31:11.370
Well, another arguing might
be, that's the wrong criterion

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.568
because the state-- when
the state just takes money

00:31:13.568 --> 00:31:15.360
from some people and
gives it to the other,

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:18.253
it isn't really
interfering with freedom.

00:31:18.253 --> 00:31:19.670
It's not-- it's
not a direct kind.

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:22.110
So you might take state
ownership of industry.

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:24.150
We have a very curious
example of this.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:31.320
If you look at the Italy
in the post-war period

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:35.250
until about 10 years ago
or so, 15 years ago, they

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:39.690
had a rather large fraction of
Italians she was state-owned.

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:41.820
This was not a deliberate
policy, by the way.

00:31:41.820 --> 00:31:43.830
In the Depression,
the fascist government

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:45.600
was bailing out
failing industries

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.790
and wound up sort
of just by accident

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:49.710
owning a very large fraction--

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.140
Olivetti, for example,
was a state-owned firm.

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:56.110
It's been privatized, but
it was a state-owned firm.

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:57.855
As far as I know,
this country-- it

00:31:57.855 --> 00:32:01.150
was never used for
political purposes.

00:32:01.150 --> 00:32:03.870
It was never an
instrument of a thing--

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:08.850
so that you had a large
fraction of state ownership

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:12.180
without any either--

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:16.650
not only did not affect
the Italian democracy,

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:18.570
but it-- that nobody
even realized it was even

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:21.790
being used in this way.

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:24.180
So I'm getting more skeptical
about the idea of state

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:26.820
ownership and things--
or actually instruments

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:31.690
by which democracy
is undermined.

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:34.640
Now, in fact, there
seem to be cases--

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.820
there seem to be some
arguments, some sense--

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:38.400
although it may be
a perverse sense--

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:43.212
state ownership may
increase democracy.

00:32:43.212 --> 00:32:44.670
One of the troubles,
of course, one

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.830
gets, if a democratic power
is sufficiently small--

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:50.820
if the power of the state is
sufficiently small, people--

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:53.080
and the United States, of
course, is a prime example--

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:56.030
may be uninterested
in participating.

00:32:56.030 --> 00:32:57.780
Now we have state
ownership, one of things

00:32:57.780 --> 00:32:59.670
you find every time you
have state ownership

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:02.010
is that the employees--

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:04.210
and managers, too,
for that matter--

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:05.680
start exerting political power.

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:08.610
It sort of energizes
the democratic system,

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:10.230
people get involved.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:12.330
Of course, not-- from an
economist point of view,

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:13.370
this is a bad thing.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:15.550
But from the point of
view of the viability,

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:19.110
there is-- it actually creates
an interest in the workings

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:20.520
of the democracy.

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:24.120
If the democratic power is
sort of itself unchallengeable,

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:26.490
then if I want higher
wages or I want

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:28.877
to keep an industry
from being closed down--

00:33:28.877 --> 00:33:30.585
you see this in Israel,
by the way, which

00:33:30.585 --> 00:33:34.320
is another state that
has until recently been

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:38.200
a high percentage
of public ownership,

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:40.710
and where the employees
are very vigorously

00:33:40.710 --> 00:33:44.010
El Al or the telephone
monopoly have

00:33:44.010 --> 00:33:46.830
been very vigorous
and effective,

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:50.190
by the way, in using
the democrat-- using

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:55.155
their power as voters to
affect government policy.

00:33:55.155 --> 00:33:57.392
Now, this may be a bad
thing, but it's certainly

00:33:57.392 --> 00:33:59.850
not a threat to democracy, it's
a threat to something else.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:04.965
School teachers in
the United States

00:34:04.965 --> 00:34:06.590
are significant--
sorry, at the local--

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:09.780
this is more at the local
level, although it's

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:12.290
entered the public
campaign also as you

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:17.060
see in the promises of
one of the candidates.

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:19.730
Another possible thing
is the role the state

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:24.739
not in actually either spending
money or in owning things,

00:34:24.739 --> 00:34:27.380
but in directing resources.

00:34:27.380 --> 00:34:30.270
Direct government controls
of one kind or another,

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:32.780
which, as Bob has said,
there's a whole continuum

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:34.440
of directions.

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:38.989
Now I find very interesting
the examples of developing

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:41.420
countries where it is
certainly the philosophy

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:45.540
of many developing countries
in the post-war period was,

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:48.870
it's hopeless for us to catch
up on a laissez faire basis.

00:34:48.870 --> 00:34:52.560
The only way we can do it
is by government direction.

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:53.561
This was very common.

00:34:53.561 --> 00:34:56.940
In fact, one might
almost say universal.

00:34:56.940 --> 00:34:59.940
Let me take India,
Korea, and Taiwan.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:03.020
In all of these countries,
the state played a major role.

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:06.330
Directed resources,
said you can't do this,

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:08.510
you can do that, this
is through by-- either

00:35:08.510 --> 00:35:14.040
by control over credit, control
over imports, various ways.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:17.245
Now India started out with
a democratic constitution,

00:35:17.245 --> 00:35:18.620
which is that it
has on the whole

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:23.390
managed to maintain even fending
off the Indira Gandhi attempt

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:27.830
to destroy it or limit it.

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:30.835
Korea and Taiwan started out
as authoritarian regimes.

00:35:30.835 --> 00:35:32.960
What's really interesting
is these state-controlled

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:33.950
regimes--

00:35:33.950 --> 00:35:36.830
in the case of India,
the democracy's

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:39.440
preserved; in the case
of Korea and Taiwan,

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:43.190
authoritarian regimes have
become democratic precisely

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:47.450
in an era when the state
was playing a major role--

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:50.630
they still play major
roles, but diminished--

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:52.380
in the economy.

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:56.820
So no matter how you
define the capitalism,

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:59.840
the correlation between
capital is more or less

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:01.930
capitalism, more or less
democracy, not the same.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:04.975
Let me take an even
more striking example--

00:36:04.975 --> 00:36:07.310
ah, haven't been watching
my time, but I think we're--

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.620
and the question, suppose you
have a democratic government

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.120
and it's capitalist.

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:19.260
Do you expect this to help
fend off a threat to democracy?

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:21.630
I've been talking here
about democracies.

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:23.940
Authoritarian regimes
becoming democratic.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:26.670
Well, we have a very-- at least
one very conspicuous case,

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:31.470
and that's Germany in 1933.

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:34.570
End the '32, beginning of '33.

00:36:34.570 --> 00:36:36.140
Here we had a pretty
well-developed

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:37.170
capitalist economy.

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:39.587
I don't think anyone would not
describe the German economy

00:36:39.587 --> 00:36:40.190
as capitalist.

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:43.250
Eh, there were a few feudal
hangovers in the east,

00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:44.180
but they were not--

00:36:44.180 --> 00:36:47.990
they didn't play-- they played
some small role in the story,

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:49.260
but I don't--

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:52.130
let me not go into details.

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:56.180
We have a squashing of
a democratic government

00:36:56.180 --> 00:37:00.315
by a dictatorship, a very--

00:37:00.315 --> 00:37:01.940
one of the most brutal
and totalitarian

00:37:01.940 --> 00:37:05.090
of all dictatorships, certainly.

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:08.140
And not only did-- not only
did capitalism not prevent it,

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:09.890
but I see no evidence
that capitalism even

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:13.010
provided any kind of a defense.

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:15.470
It's not even that they
lost the war, somehow.

00:37:15.470 --> 00:37:17.840
It was-- like the capitalists,
the fact that it was

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.750
a capitalist country seemed
to play no role whatever

00:37:20.750 --> 00:37:21.980
in the transform--

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:24.170
the Hiterite take over.

00:37:24.170 --> 00:37:30.740
So it's-- so I say, the idea
that capitalism provides any

00:37:30.740 --> 00:37:31.790
kind of--

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:35.990
that in principle, it provides
any kind of protection.

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:38.540
Then we can go, the
question, can capitalism

00:37:38.540 --> 00:37:40.092
be a threat to democracy?

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:46.700
Well-- and Bob has certainly
touched on that question--

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:50.290
essentially the problem
may be that democracy--

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:52.790
when you have political power
divorced from economic power--

00:37:52.790 --> 00:37:55.270
this is going back way
to that quotation Marx

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:57.555
that Professor Cohen cited.

00:37:57.555 --> 00:38:01.160
If capitalism-- the equality--

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:03.980
political power is in
the hands of a majority,

00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.590
and it may well be
that it will take steps

00:38:06.590 --> 00:38:09.610
which are anti-capitalist.

00:38:09.610 --> 00:38:12.940
And when it does, say, against
the owners of capital--

00:38:12.940 --> 00:38:16.660
against those who
have profited by--

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:19.900
those who have most profited
most by the capitalist regime.

00:38:19.900 --> 00:38:21.940
Of course, as Bob
says, one thing

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:26.020
we have in a capitalist regime
is very considerable inequality

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:27.110
I gotta say one thing.

00:38:27.110 --> 00:38:29.220
I think what happens
in every regime is

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:30.220
considerable inequality.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.500
I'm not sure that capitalism
is any particularly

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:37.930
distinguished in this respect,
but the socialist regimes

00:38:37.930 --> 00:38:40.850
have in fact considerable
income inequality.

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:42.336
But whatever it is, you have--

00:38:42.336 --> 00:38:44.253
in other words,
there is equality,

00:38:44.253 --> 00:38:46.420
and very considerable
inequality in the capitalist--

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:49.420
in a capitalist regime.

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.790
And then, of course,
you have a divergence

00:38:51.790 --> 00:38:52.850
between economic power.

00:38:52.850 --> 00:38:55.720
Economic power is translatable
to political power.

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:58.460
The United States, we're
thinking of buying the power,

00:38:58.460 --> 00:39:01.585
but there are other ways,
like buying military power

00:39:01.585 --> 00:39:03.085
instead of interfering
in democrat--

00:39:03.085 --> 00:39:05.220
and that's, of course,
we have in Chile.

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:07.340
1973.

00:39:07.340 --> 00:39:10.860
We had it in Spain in 1936.

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:12.210
Very much the same story.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.048
A left-wing government comes
in with a big so-- not--

00:39:15.048 --> 00:39:16.590
wasn't by any means
purely socialist.

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:19.050
As a matter of fact, it
had socialist and communist

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:20.160
elements in it.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:22.240
Immediately there's
a counter-revolution.

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:24.240
In this case, it's because--
in both those cases

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:29.250
because of the fact that there
was a strong military power.

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:31.860
Why that's-- why the military
is kind of a third power,

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:34.590
and that's-- why that's
a separate power in some

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:37.130
countries and not in others
is something I don't--

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:38.790
is a fact, but I don't know why.

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:41.500
I don't have any
explanations for that.

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:44.505
So that's a case-- those are
cases, at least, in which--

00:39:44.505 --> 00:39:47.887
and of course, Latin
America will exemplify that

00:39:47.887 --> 00:39:48.720
over and over again.

00:39:52.374 --> 00:39:55.830
Those are cases in which
capital-- that which--

00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:58.470
in fact, a capitalist interest.

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:01.380
Sometimes it's even particular
capitalist interest rather than

00:40:01.380 --> 00:40:03.990
a general capitalist interest.

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:08.140
Can produce a
subversion of democracy,

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:11.340
or capitalism may actually
be a danger to democracy.

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:15.600
To be fair about it, I think
that almost any power structure

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:16.340
in this--

00:40:16.340 --> 00:40:18.270
say in a civil society,
a non-government--

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:20.550
a non-political--
which differs strongly,

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:23.010
which is powerful
somehow, and which

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:26.610
differs from the
political majority

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:28.860
can lead to similar situations.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:32.225
In the modern world, probably
ethnic considerations

00:40:32.225 --> 00:40:33.600
have played some
of the same role

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:35.820
that I'm describing
here to capitalism.

00:40:35.820 --> 00:40:39.377
But capitalism is one
of the dangers in this--

00:40:39.377 --> 00:40:40.335
in particular contexts.

00:40:43.120 --> 00:40:48.000
So since-- it's not very clear
as to why capitalism is related

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:50.150
to democracy in
any permissive way

00:40:50.150 --> 00:40:53.230
according to these
historical accounts.

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:56.540
And as-- on the
other hand, there

00:40:56.540 --> 00:40:58.170
does seem to be an association.

00:40:58.170 --> 00:40:59.820
Of course, I-- our evidences--

00:40:59.820 --> 00:41:02.460
I should be careful-- our
evidence in some sense

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:06.390
is dominated by one
or perhaps two cases,

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:08.820
namely the Soviet
Union and China.

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:11.790
Eastern Europe I don't count,
because Eastern Europe is,

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:15.180
to a very large-- mainly
on the simply the--

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:16.350
Soviet tanks.

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:19.410
There's no great mystery as
to why East Germany and Poland

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:23.730
and Romania and so forth
became a communist country--

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:25.630
Yugoslavia is a bit of
an exception that way.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:28.290
They've got their-- its
own indigenous things

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:31.820
and that's a complicated story.

00:41:31.820 --> 00:41:33.510
In Czechoslovakia,
there was, in fact,

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:36.543
a strong communist
movement, but not

00:41:36.543 --> 00:41:37.710
true of any other countries.

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:40.290
So Hungary last free election
gave the communists 10%

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:42.615
of the vote Now we
could-- we have--

00:41:42.615 --> 00:41:45.690
and the whole-- and even
Czechoslovakia was quite--

00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:49.230
the communists in Czechoslovakia
we know very well moved--

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:51.450
as those in Hungary,
too, for that matter--

00:41:51.450 --> 00:41:56.070
moved against-- even a
dissidence within the communist

00:41:56.070 --> 00:42:00.090
period could not-- was not
tolerated as we know in 1953

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:00.810
and--

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:07.120
1956 and 1968, or 1953 in
Berlin, as a matter of fact.

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:12.180
The-- so I don't
really count those

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:15.236
as examples of
capitalism-- having to do

00:42:15.236 --> 00:42:16.640
with capitalism or democracy.

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:18.030
In the Soviet Union--

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:19.290
but of course, the interesting
thing about the Soviet Union

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:21.930
and China, since capitalism
could hardly have been said

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.270
to have been a well-developed
phenomenon either of those

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:25.597
countries-- it was--

00:42:25.597 --> 00:42:27.930
I don't know-- there are
capitalist elements, all right,

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:30.388
but there was-- there was a
lot of non-capitalist elements.

00:42:30.388 --> 00:42:33.000
So that's why I say there's
some difficulty finding--

00:42:33.000 --> 00:42:34.500
I mean, capitalism--
but I think one

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:38.610
would agree that pre-World
War I Russia was not

00:42:38.610 --> 00:42:40.570
a great example of a
well-developed capitalist

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:41.070
system.

00:42:41.070 --> 00:42:44.700
There were sectors of
the country, and so China

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:46.860
even less so.

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:49.280
So that the take-- the
communist takeovers there were--

00:42:49.280 --> 00:42:52.320
are not much evidence on the
question of the relationship.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:53.440
Let's see.

00:42:53.440 --> 00:42:56.250
So in a sense,
our identification

00:42:56.250 --> 00:43:00.930
of socialism with dictatorship
and capitalism with democracy

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.760
in a way fails, but
of course, there

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:05.500
are not many examples
of the former,

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:06.900
and they're rather special.

00:43:06.900 --> 00:43:08.610
Then on the latter,
there are a lot,

00:43:08.610 --> 00:43:10.443
of course, as I've said
over and over again,

00:43:10.443 --> 00:43:13.810
authoritarian capitalist
states, nobody denies that.

00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:16.020
Not even the most
laissez faire economist.

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:20.930
Milton Friedman agrees
to that statement.

00:43:20.930 --> 00:43:22.960
So another point of view--

00:43:22.960 --> 00:43:25.290
for whatever association
there is is that really,

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:27.210
there is a third variable here--

00:43:27.210 --> 00:43:29.793
economic growth.

00:43:29.793 --> 00:43:31.210
Speaking or
summarizing a lecture,

00:43:31.210 --> 00:43:33.900
there is of a theory
sometimes called

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:36.933
the Aristotle-Lipset hypothesis.

00:43:36.933 --> 00:43:38.100
I think there's some quote--

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:41.430
Aristotle, of course,
interpreting--

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:44.730
Aristotle in modern
terms is always

00:43:44.730 --> 00:43:47.550
at best a tricky
business and contentious,

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:49.450
because the circumstances
were so different,

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:52.010
but there are some ideas
apparently, Aristotle

00:43:52.010 --> 00:43:55.890
and then Seymour Martin
Lipset put forth some view

00:43:55.890 --> 00:44:02.670
many years ago that people
taking cross-sections and doing

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:05.220
statistical analysis
claim to find this,

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:10.090
that essentially economic
growth leads to democracy.

00:44:10.090 --> 00:44:14.320
And we have tended to view
that economic growth is itself

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:16.860
stimulate-- or
capitalism is itself--

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:19.420
so you see a major
enterprise in the development

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:23.300
of economic-- a major
cause of economic growth.

00:44:23.300 --> 00:44:26.570
So this suggests that whatever
association-- now, by the way,

00:44:26.570 --> 00:44:29.320
I mentioned these as
hypotheses are advanced

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:30.695
and seriously studied.

00:44:30.695 --> 00:44:33.070
The way they say they approved
I think would be nonsense,

00:44:33.070 --> 00:44:34.600
and of course, one
thing is clear--

00:44:34.600 --> 00:44:38.620
if you fit statistically the
variance around the fitted

00:44:38.620 --> 00:44:39.970
lines, it's pretty--

00:44:39.970 --> 00:44:42.820
it's very, very big.

00:44:42.820 --> 00:44:45.850
For example, there was one
big cross-section analysis

00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:46.920
designed--

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:48.580
due to Robert Barrow.

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:51.370
And I-- whereas I'm
a theorist and I look

00:44:51.370 --> 00:44:53.050
at the theory of the model.

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:53.950
I looked at the--

00:44:53.950 --> 00:44:56.050
he actually tabulated
the residuals,

00:44:56.050 --> 00:44:59.580
and China was way off.

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:02.770
The economic-- or the
democracy in China was--

00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:04.720
registered a very low
level, well below what

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:07.520
would have predicted by the
economic growth of China.

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:10.900
So even if there's some vague
statistical associations,

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:13.300
like a lot of these
other associations,

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.760
like the alleged relationship
between intelligence

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:18.040
and income, which-- it's there.

00:45:18.040 --> 00:45:19.000
It's perfectly real.

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:23.043
It's just not as big-- as
nearly as big as people think.

00:45:23.043 --> 00:45:24.960
And the evidence--
statistical evidence there,

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:26.330
unlike a country--

00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:27.945
comparing countries
is really dubious.

00:45:27.945 --> 00:45:30.320
In the case of the intelligence,
I think that we really--

00:45:30.320 --> 00:45:33.430
we have much better data, and
the evidence is very striking.

00:45:36.290 --> 00:45:37.610
Now we're not likely to get--

00:45:37.610 --> 00:45:39.820
so the hypothesis we're
discussing today, in a way,

00:45:39.820 --> 00:45:41.950
it's not likely to get
a test because everybody

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:44.620
says they're capitalists today.

00:45:44.620 --> 00:45:48.192
We got to-- I mean, we
can discuss, as Bob and--

00:45:48.192 --> 00:45:49.900
not surprised, those
who know us will not

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:53.094
be surprised that my political
opinions are not very--

00:45:53.094 --> 00:45:55.840
are hardly different
from those of Bob.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:59.100
We sign a lot of
statements together.

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:01.700
So I don't want to repeat
his points, but of course,

00:46:01.700 --> 00:46:05.732
I think it's quite
clear, and I won't--

00:46:05.732 --> 00:46:07.940
that the-- from the point
of view of economic growth,

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:12.080
which is at least
helpful in democracy--

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:14.740
I don't know that we
know any more than that--

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:19.030
is that the capitalism itself
doesn't work very well when

00:46:19.030 --> 00:46:22.270
it's not well-regulated
and not proper--

00:46:22.270 --> 00:46:24.760
when there isn't the checks
and balances which can only

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:27.340
supplied by a democracy,
a democratic--

00:46:27.340 --> 00:46:30.040
powerful democratic state with--

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:34.065
and access to free
information by all citizens.

00:46:34.065 --> 00:46:35.550
Thank you.

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:39.015
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:45.028 --> 00:46:46.570
SUZANNE BERGER:
Well, our topic today

00:46:46.570 --> 00:46:49.750
is, is capitalism
good for democracy?

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:52.630
But as a political
scientist, I guess

00:46:52.630 --> 00:46:55.420
I think the great
surprise in human history

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:59.650
is how good democracy
has been for capitalism.

00:46:59.650 --> 00:47:03.610
And the great surprise has
been that even in societies

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:07.480
in which capitalism has
generated enormous inequalities

00:47:07.480 --> 00:47:11.840
of property, wealth,
wages, and opportunities,

00:47:11.840 --> 00:47:14.950
the majority does not
vote to confiscate

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:17.560
the riches of the super rich.

00:47:17.560 --> 00:47:22.780
The majority does not vote to
flatten income inequalities

00:47:22.780 --> 00:47:26.560
through the tax system, let
alone to overturn property

00:47:26.560 --> 00:47:29.633
rights or the market economy.

00:47:29.633 --> 00:47:31.300
And what I'd like to
talk about with you

00:47:31.300 --> 00:47:35.170
this afternoon is why
capitalism and democracy have

00:47:35.170 --> 00:47:39.340
been compatible, and
whether this compatibility

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:43.240
can survive the
globalization of capitalism.

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:47.260
As we see waves of
cross-border flows of capital,

00:47:47.260 --> 00:47:51.340
goods, and services
transforming our economies,

00:47:51.340 --> 00:47:55.120
what's going to happen to
the fit between capitalism

00:47:55.120 --> 00:47:57.550
and democracy?

00:47:57.550 --> 00:48:01.090
Over the past 200 years,
more and more countries

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:05.410
have come to have both liberal
democratic governments in which

00:48:05.410 --> 00:48:08.980
they choose leaders in
competitive elections,

00:48:08.980 --> 00:48:12.220
and at the same time,
free market economies

00:48:12.220 --> 00:48:14.470
with private property rights.

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:20.530
These two systems have coexisted
with remarkable stability

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:24.580
despite the inequalities
that capitalism generates.

00:48:24.580 --> 00:48:27.910
No electorate anywhere
that I know of

00:48:27.910 --> 00:48:33.220
has ever voted in free elections
to overturn capitalism.

00:48:33.220 --> 00:48:36.610
There have been very strong
anti-capitalist political

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:40.840
movements of both left and
right in Europe and in Asia

00:48:40.840 --> 00:48:42.610
and in the United
States, indeed,

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:46.150
but where political
change has taken place

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:48.480
through free and
democratic elections,

00:48:48.480 --> 00:48:52.810
anti-capitalism has
never won the day.

00:48:52.810 --> 00:48:59.080
Now this result that democracy
and capitalism can co-exist

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:01.460
was not always
taken for granted.

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:05.080
I don't think you need to
have been a Marxist or Marx

00:49:05.080 --> 00:49:09.250
to have had a great doubt about
the potential compatibility

00:49:09.250 --> 00:49:11.150
of democracy and capitalism.

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:13.000
In fact, it was
the great anxiety

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:15.490
of the Founders of
the American republic

00:49:15.490 --> 00:49:18.700
that democratic
politics might trample

00:49:18.700 --> 00:49:20.770
the rights of the propertied.

00:49:20.770 --> 00:49:23.590
To take the formulation of
one of the Founding Fathers,

00:49:23.590 --> 00:49:26.800
James Madison, he writes
in the Federalist Papers,

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:29.080
the great danger
in a democracy is

00:49:29.080 --> 00:49:31.780
that citizens might organize,
they might mobilize,

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:35.590
they might form a faction to
push their economic interests

00:49:35.590 --> 00:49:37.540
against property-holders.

00:49:37.540 --> 00:49:40.480
And here-- I'm
quoting Madison now--

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:43.570
"Democracies have
ever been spectacles

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:46.180
of turbulence and contention.

00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:49.210
They have ever been
found incompatible

00:49:49.210 --> 00:49:53.380
with personal security or
the rights of property.

00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:57.730
In general, democracies have
been as short in their lives

00:49:57.730 --> 00:50:01.420
as they have been
violent in their deaths."

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:03.190
So I think the
question we might start

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:05.650
with is why these
dire predictions

00:50:05.650 --> 00:50:09.610
about democratic government
and an economic system based

00:50:09.610 --> 00:50:13.300
on individual property rights
and on a free market economy

00:50:13.300 --> 00:50:16.450
have proved wrong, and I
think there are basically

00:50:16.450 --> 00:50:17.840
two reasons.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:21.580
First, the constitutional
engineering of Madison

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:25.030
and the founders of other
liberal democratic societies

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:29.020
did, in fact, work to protect
the rights of individuals

00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:31.570
and the functioning
of a market economy.

00:50:31.570 --> 00:50:34.450
Institutions like the Bill
of Rights, the Supreme Court,

00:50:34.450 --> 00:50:38.230
federalism, and much
else did in fact

00:50:38.230 --> 00:50:43.090
build dikes that protect
property and protect markets

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:46.342
against democratic majorities.

00:50:46.342 --> 00:50:47.800
And secondly, and
this is the point

00:50:47.800 --> 00:50:49.930
I want to focus
on this afternoon,

00:50:49.930 --> 00:50:53.140
democracy and capitalism
have been compatible

00:50:53.140 --> 00:50:56.530
all these years, I think,
because until recently,

00:50:56.530 --> 00:51:00.040
capitalism had been
largely contained

00:51:00.040 --> 00:51:02.410
within national boundaries.

00:51:02.410 --> 00:51:04.960
Governments still
stood on the frontiers

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:07.210
of their national
economies, and they

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:11.200
regulated the flow of labor,
capital goods, and services

00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:15.670
between their own societies
and the international economy.

00:51:15.670 --> 00:51:18.100
Within domestic
societies, governments

00:51:18.100 --> 00:51:20.860
acted to cushion the
most disruptive features

00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:22.120
of capitalism.

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:25.930
Business cycles, unemployment,
inflation, depression,

00:51:25.930 --> 00:51:28.330
environmental degradation.

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:31.090
Now of course, some liberal
democratic governments

00:51:31.090 --> 00:51:33.280
buffered and
regulated capitalism

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:36.980
more than others, and in
some times more than others.

00:51:36.980 --> 00:51:39.550
But what strikes me is
that in the United States,

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:43.500
both Republicans and Democrats,
like Social Democrats

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:46.440
and Scandinavia, like
the Tories in Britain,

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:50.820
all acknowledge some measure
of government's responsibility

00:51:50.820 --> 00:51:54.750
and government's capability
for regulating capitalism

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:57.870
to mitigate its
negative effects.

00:51:57.870 --> 00:52:00.720
To put it another way,
the potential clash

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:03.960
between democracy
and capitalism was

00:52:03.960 --> 00:52:08.400
averted by constitutions that
made our democracies something

00:52:08.400 --> 00:52:12.120
other than pure democracies,
and by politics that

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:15.840
made our capitalism something
different than the textbook

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:18.240
models of capitalism.

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:22.830
Now today, I think globalization
threatens to undermine

00:52:22.830 --> 00:52:25.490
this historic compromise.

00:52:25.490 --> 00:52:29.460
And by globalization, I
mean simply the emergence

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:32.280
of a single world
market and a single set

00:52:32.280 --> 00:52:36.300
of prices for goods,
services, capital, and labor.

00:52:36.300 --> 00:52:38.010
And of course, there
are many changes

00:52:38.010 --> 00:52:41.520
that are driving this forward,
new technologies, the fall

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:44.520
of the Berlin Wall, the
worldwide liberalization

00:52:44.520 --> 00:52:49.590
of financial markets, the rise
of big new consumer markets,

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:54.120
the rise of big new producers
outside the old developed

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:55.740
world.

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:59.250
Globalization means that
more of the population

00:52:59.250 --> 00:53:04.020
everywhere becomes more
vulnerable to economic forces

00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:07.090
outside their own country.

00:53:07.090 --> 00:53:09.690
It means a widening
of the inequalities

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:14.340
that we've already seen
grow in our own society.

00:53:14.340 --> 00:53:18.030
And at the same time,
globalization apparently

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:21.000
reduces government's
capabilities

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:25.570
for shielding citizens
against markets.

00:53:25.570 --> 00:53:28.110
In fact, far from
shielding citizens

00:53:28.110 --> 00:53:32.970
against the harshest blows
of globalization, today,

00:53:32.970 --> 00:53:36.780
governments' credibility
with international investors

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:39.960
seems to depend on
governments' willingness

00:53:39.960 --> 00:53:44.430
to subject citizens to the
full force of market pressures.

00:53:44.430 --> 00:53:48.300
So for example, in the Asian
financial crisis, South Korea,

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:51.000
Thailand, Hong
Kong, they virtually

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:54.240
competed in making
commitments to impose

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:57.600
the burdens of adjustment
on their own citizens

00:53:57.600 --> 00:54:00.810
without regard to
their relative ability

00:54:00.810 --> 00:54:05.451
or their relative willingness
to absorb the pain.

00:54:05.451 --> 00:54:07.770
Now I think the result
in our own country

00:54:07.770 --> 00:54:12.840
and abroad has been the
growing fear of globalization.

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:15.480
The protesters in
Seattle and Prague

00:54:15.480 --> 00:54:19.140
may look like marginal
and violent individuals,

00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:23.040
but it's a fact that their
slogans make a lot of sense

00:54:23.040 --> 00:54:27.420
to many ordinary
mainstream citizens.

00:54:27.420 --> 00:54:29.520
It's a stunning
fact to me that even

00:54:29.520 --> 00:54:32.520
after years of economic
growth and prosperity

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:36.480
in the United States, that
surveys find that over 50%

00:54:36.480 --> 00:54:42.630
of people say that free trade
is bad for the United States.

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:44.730
Now what this reflects
is undoubtedly

00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:48.090
not economic
sophistication, but it

00:54:48.090 --> 00:54:52.590
is a measure of the
growing disease, the sense

00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:56.880
of a loss of control over
the basic foundations

00:54:56.880 --> 00:54:59.160
of societal well-being.

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:02.430
And I think many people
fear that there's

00:55:02.430 --> 00:55:07.140
no one who can be held
accountable for basic choices

00:55:07.140 --> 00:55:10.570
about society's use of
resources and society's

00:55:10.570 --> 00:55:14.520
allocations of reward and risk.

00:55:14.520 --> 00:55:18.210
When citizens ceased to believe
that they together with others

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:23.070
can shape their own future,
then liberal democracies

00:55:23.070 --> 00:55:24.550
are in trouble.

00:55:24.550 --> 00:55:26.970
And I think that's the
fundamental challenge

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:28.530
we face today.

00:55:28.530 --> 00:55:32.160
It's not only an issue of
how to protect weak groups

00:55:32.160 --> 00:55:36.150
in our society, even though
that is very important,

00:55:36.150 --> 00:55:38.940
but I think that even
more difficult question

00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:42.210
in a world with an
international economy

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:48.030
but no international government
is how to bring globalization

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:52.470
into the domain of human
choice and human decision?

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:55.590
How to bring globalization
into the domain

00:55:55.590 --> 00:55:58.540
of liberal democratic process?

00:55:58.540 --> 00:56:01.320
How can we make the
globalization, how

00:56:01.320 --> 00:56:04.740
can we make capitalism
in a global economy

00:56:04.740 --> 00:56:08.310
compatible with
liberal democracy?

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:10.950
Now I think one
solution to this dilemma

00:56:10.950 --> 00:56:15.300
could be to provide individuals
with resources that would allow

00:56:15.300 --> 00:56:19.470
each person to protect
himself in the new economy,

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:23.580
and that way, we would reduce
the burden of expectations

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:26.040
on what governments need to do.

00:56:26.040 --> 00:56:28.530
What kind of resources would
we want people to have?

00:56:28.530 --> 00:56:31.590
Well, the kind of
education and training

00:56:31.590 --> 00:56:34.410
that would allow a person
to reinvent herself

00:56:34.410 --> 00:56:37.560
in her job over the
course of a lifetime.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:41.490
We'd want people to be able to
diversify their assets so as

00:56:41.490 --> 00:56:45.160
to spread family risks,
so that for example,

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:48.480
an average family's life
savings and wealth would not

00:56:48.480 --> 00:56:52.530
be so heavily concentrated
in the ownership of a house,

00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:55.650
a home, whose value
depends on many factors

00:56:55.650 --> 00:56:58.540
outside an individual
or the family's control.

00:56:58.540 --> 00:57:03.210
And I think such policies would
be major gains in our society,

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:07.150
and they're policies on
which most of us can agree.

00:57:07.150 --> 00:57:11.740
But even if we were to imagine
the best of education systems,

00:57:11.740 --> 00:57:15.060
even if we were to add a
kind of minimum safety net

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:17.400
for those people who
are not intelligent

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:20.520
enough or not agile
enough to take advantage

00:57:20.520 --> 00:57:23.070
of these opportunities,
I think there still

00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:26.160
remains a big question,
and the big question

00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:30.030
is, are citizens in a
democracy willing to accept

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:33.380
that the basic social
distributions of risk,

00:57:33.380 --> 00:57:38.430
resources, and uncertainties
move so far beyond their reach,

00:57:38.430 --> 00:57:42.690
move so far beyond
democratic accountability?

00:57:42.690 --> 00:57:47.130
And on this question,
diagnoses and prescriptions

00:57:47.130 --> 00:57:49.750
obviously diverge widely.

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:51.390
Some people think
there's nothing

00:57:51.390 --> 00:57:54.990
that government can do with the
national or international level

00:57:54.990 --> 00:57:57.720
to regulate globalization.

00:57:57.720 --> 00:58:01.530
Thomas Friedman, who writes on
the op-ed page of The New York

00:58:01.530 --> 00:58:04.140
Times, put it in a
very striking way.

00:58:04.140 --> 00:58:06.840
He describes a
globalized economy

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:10.030
as a race of Formula 1 cars.

00:58:10.030 --> 00:58:12.690
And he says, if you're
worried about drivers smashing

00:58:12.690 --> 00:58:16.020
into the walls, what you can
do is give them better driving

00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:19.830
lessons and improve the skills
of the ambulance technicians

00:58:19.830 --> 00:58:23.010
to clean up after the wrecks.

00:58:23.010 --> 00:58:26.430
But, says Friedman, you
can't put bales of straw

00:58:26.430 --> 00:58:29.190
all around the walls
of the racetrack

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:34.320
to buffer the accidents, or else
you're going to ruin the race.

00:58:34.320 --> 00:58:37.200
If you don't want to
be a Formula 1 driver,

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:40.950
then walk or bicycle,
but watch out--

00:58:40.950 --> 00:58:45.930
you're going to be run
over by a Formula 1 driver.

00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:49.560
Now as a professor at
MIT for many years,

00:58:49.560 --> 00:58:52.470
I've devoted all my
professional life

00:58:52.470 --> 00:58:55.950
to educating Formula 1 drivers.

00:58:55.950 --> 00:58:57.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:57.690 --> 00:59:01.650
I know how much they
contribute to the giant leaps

00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:06.520
forward in science, technology,
and human organization that

00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:11.630
are transforming human
well-being around the world.

00:59:11.630 --> 00:59:12.780
And yet, I believe--

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:16.680
and I know from discussions with
my students-- that most of them

00:59:16.680 --> 00:59:19.920
believe that we want
to live in societies

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:24.870
in which the basic rules about
the game, the basic rules

00:59:24.870 --> 00:59:30.480
about the race are decided not
just by and for the fastest

00:59:30.480 --> 00:59:33.850
drivers, but by all that people.

00:59:33.850 --> 00:59:37.080
And our problem, like
James Madison's, is

00:59:37.080 --> 00:59:40.110
to find institutions
that all allow

00:59:40.110 --> 00:59:44.910
us to reconcile democratic
control and capitalism.

00:59:44.910 --> 00:59:47.880
That capitalism
today is global makes

00:59:47.880 --> 00:59:53.220
this the most difficult and
important of challenges.

00:59:53.220 --> 00:59:56.510
MIT, as I have known
it, is a university

00:59:56.510 --> 00:59:59.610
driven by real-world
problems, and I

00:59:59.610 --> 01:00:03.210
think here we have one
that deserves our best

01:00:03.210 --> 01:00:05.850
thinking and imagination.

01:00:05.850 --> 01:00:09.930
And it's my hope for MIT
and my hope for the one

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:14.430
to grow on for the school that
we can bring our Formula 1

01:00:14.430 --> 01:00:19.360
faculty and students together
to respond to this challenge.

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:20.340
Thank you.

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:23.770
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:38.960 --> 01:00:42.598
AUDIENCE: My comment
is directed at--

01:00:42.598 --> 01:00:43.348
AUDIENCE: Wait up.

01:00:55.940 --> 01:00:59.000
AUDIENCE: My comment is directed
primarily at Professor Arrow.

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:03.380
I was listening
very interestingly

01:01:03.380 --> 01:01:06.710
and finding myself
in the funny position

01:01:06.710 --> 01:01:11.110
of agreeing with
his point of view

01:01:11.110 --> 01:01:14.930
while disagreeing vehemently
with one of the examples.

01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:20.220
I grew up in Italy
in the '50s and '60s,

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:23.930
and I can guarantee you
that that concentration

01:01:23.930 --> 01:01:27.420
of economic power was
indeed corrupting.

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:32.600
It indeed generated a lot
of examples of everything

01:01:32.600 --> 01:01:38.870
from the most base graft to
the very programmed and planned

01:01:38.870 --> 01:01:42.320
usage of millions of
votes of individuals

01:01:42.320 --> 01:01:44.660
who would benefit
from that system

01:01:44.660 --> 01:01:48.980
in order to support
a political system.

01:01:48.980 --> 01:01:49.880
However--

01:01:49.880 --> 01:01:53.450
KENNETH ARROW: You're part of
the Institute of [INAUDIBLE]??

01:01:53.450 --> 01:01:54.250
AUDIENCE: No.

01:01:54.250 --> 01:01:56.025
KENNETH ARROW: That's
what I had in mind.

01:01:56.025 --> 01:01:57.650
AUDIENCE: I'm thinking
of [INAUDIBLE],,

01:01:57.650 --> 01:02:01.100
I'm thinking of [INAUDIBLE],,
I'm thinking of [INAUDIBLE]

01:02:01.100 --> 01:02:03.080
and of various others.

01:02:03.080 --> 01:02:10.160
However, it didn't work in
the direction of tyranny,

01:02:10.160 --> 01:02:13.410
primarily because
as a population,

01:02:13.410 --> 01:02:16.820
we tend to prefer to discuss
these things over a good meal

01:02:16.820 --> 01:02:21.170
with a lot of wine, and that
tends to calm the spirits

01:02:21.170 --> 01:02:24.740
and to focus on
important things in life.

01:02:24.740 --> 01:02:29.730
But I do want to say that
over 15 years in this country,

01:02:29.730 --> 01:02:32.300
one of the items that I
find most interesting--

01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:37.460
and I think the topic of today's
symposium is a symbol of it--

01:02:37.460 --> 01:02:40.940
is a peculiar
obsession with thinking

01:02:40.940 --> 01:02:45.860
that capitalism and democracy
are indeed connected.

01:02:45.860 --> 01:02:49.490
And I'm wondering if the
question shouldn't be,

01:02:49.490 --> 01:02:52.090
what else besides
the economic system,

01:02:52.090 --> 01:02:56.030
be that capitalism or
socialism, is indeed

01:02:56.030 --> 01:03:00.440
conducive to the kind of
remarkably open society

01:03:00.440 --> 01:03:02.960
which I think this
country is one of the best

01:03:02.960 --> 01:03:05.047
examples in the world?

01:03:05.047 --> 01:03:07.130
JOSHUA COHEN: It's not the
quality of the cuisine.

01:03:07.130 --> 01:03:10.483
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:16.153
KENNETH ARROW: Well, thank
you for your comment.

01:03:16.153 --> 01:03:17.820
There is, of course,
an important point,

01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:21.800
which I alluded to in one form,
but I didn't go into detail,

01:03:21.800 --> 01:03:25.420
which is, when you have
an economy, let's say--

01:03:25.420 --> 01:03:29.930
let's say a capitalist economy
in conjunction with a political

01:03:29.930 --> 01:03:34.240
system which has
economic power--

01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:38.980
state ownership,
regulatory behavior--

01:03:38.980 --> 01:03:41.380
you have, of course, the
question of translating

01:03:41.380 --> 01:03:42.410
that power into--

01:03:42.410 --> 01:03:44.535
the economic power translates
into political power,

01:03:44.535 --> 01:03:47.960
and one form takes, of course,
is straight corruption.

01:03:47.960 --> 01:03:49.960
As you suggest, it can
be used by the government

01:03:49.960 --> 01:03:53.380
to try to buy votes I think
it was one of your points--

01:03:53.380 --> 01:03:57.220
it could be used for personal
gain to get privileges

01:03:57.220 --> 01:03:58.870
and so forth.

01:03:58.870 --> 01:04:02.800
That is, of course, a
fundamental dilemma of which

01:04:02.800 --> 01:04:03.940
there's no way of avoiding.

01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:06.640
Now one argument, of course,
this does lead to an argument

01:04:06.640 --> 01:04:10.330
for a very minimal state, which
I think, unfortunately has,

01:04:10.330 --> 01:04:14.760
other problems, that the
inequality that arise in--

01:04:14.760 --> 01:04:16.840
and even inefficiencies
that arise out

01:04:16.840 --> 01:04:19.510
of an unregulated capitalism.

01:04:19.510 --> 01:04:21.310
Also, involve--
create another set

01:04:21.310 --> 01:04:23.810
of problems that are different
from the corruption problems.

01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:30.570
I think maybe it was Professor
Cohen in his direct remarks

01:04:30.570 --> 01:04:31.547
suggested there's--

01:04:31.547 --> 01:04:33.130
there are always--
it's not a question

01:04:33.130 --> 01:04:35.600
of saying that you
will avoid problems.

01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:39.045
It's a question of saying, we
try to minimize the tensions.

01:04:39.045 --> 01:04:41.170
Indeed, one can argue that
if you have no problems,

01:04:41.170 --> 01:04:42.878
you have no progress,
either, by the way,

01:04:42.878 --> 01:04:45.593
so that the tensions
can be fruitful.

01:04:45.593 --> 01:04:47.760
All I was pointing out in
Italy, it was-- one thing,

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:50.260
it was not useful, and that was
the only thing I was saying,

01:04:50.260 --> 01:04:53.940
that it affected the
democratic system--

01:04:53.940 --> 01:04:56.443
it affected the performance of
the democratic system I think

01:04:56.443 --> 01:04:58.360
is what you're saying,
not-- it did not affect

01:04:58.360 --> 01:05:01.750
the democratic structure.

01:05:01.750 --> 01:05:04.000
And this is, of course,
a problem can occur

01:05:04.000 --> 01:05:06.820
in vote buying of all sorts.

01:05:06.820 --> 01:05:09.110
One typical example
in the United States--

01:05:09.110 --> 01:05:11.260
it's not anything
threatening to democracy,

01:05:11.260 --> 01:05:13.060
but it's a complication--

01:05:13.060 --> 01:05:20.190
is military expenditures
as local goods,

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:23.470
as providing an income
and local goods.

01:05:23.470 --> 01:05:26.740
The most extreme version
is we have bases.

01:05:26.740 --> 01:05:28.590
These bases typically
develop a relationship

01:05:28.590 --> 01:05:30.700
to the local economy
or thought of--

01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:32.700
probably not even correctly,
but they're thought

01:05:32.700 --> 01:05:35.530
of as supporting the economy.

01:05:35.530 --> 01:05:38.220
Anytime you try-- the Defense
Department is perfectly willing

01:05:38.220 --> 01:05:39.810
to have these bases closed.

01:05:39.810 --> 01:05:41.393
They have no interest
in many of them.

01:05:41.393 --> 01:05:43.060
It's not even-- it's
not even a question

01:05:43.060 --> 01:05:44.620
of a military
industrial complex.

01:05:44.620 --> 01:05:46.480
The military don't care.

01:05:46.480 --> 01:05:48.480
In fact, they'd like the
money, but they want it

01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:49.500
for other purposes.

01:05:52.110 --> 01:05:55.140
But then you always
have local senators,

01:05:55.140 --> 01:05:58.137
congressmen and so forth
protecting their interests.

01:05:58.137 --> 01:05:59.595
So as soon as you
have-- and that's

01:05:59.595 --> 01:06:02.010
an example where there's
some economic power

01:06:02.010 --> 01:06:05.100
operating in some locality,
and therefore generates

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:05.760
local things.

01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:08.560
And the Texas oil
millionaires and the--

01:06:08.560 --> 01:06:11.850
all of these-- the
continuous buying

01:06:11.850 --> 01:06:16.020
of special tax provisions,
which is notorious, all of these

01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:19.170
are examples of when as soon as
the state has economic power,

01:06:19.170 --> 01:06:23.010
it's likely to lead to an
attempt to buy that power.

01:06:23.010 --> 01:06:25.630
And you point out-- it's quite
correct that I'm sure that

01:06:25.630 --> 01:06:29.190
the [INAUDIBLE] and any
involve themselves in--

01:06:29.190 --> 01:06:30.450
I mean, I don't know the--

01:06:30.450 --> 01:06:32.910
I'll take your word for
the facts, but it's--

01:06:32.910 --> 01:06:35.520
logically I see no argument
against your position

01:06:35.520 --> 01:06:36.240
that they--

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:38.910
once you have these powers, that
you will-- they will be used,

01:06:38.910 --> 01:06:39.570
yes.

01:06:39.570 --> 01:06:41.273
And the only hope
would be to create

01:06:41.273 --> 01:06:42.690
competition and
other things which

01:06:42.690 --> 01:06:45.855
would make this unsustainable.

01:06:48.732 --> 01:06:49.815
JOSHUA COHEN: In the back.

01:06:59.715 --> 01:07:00.692
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:07:00.692 --> 01:07:02.400
This is kind of for
anybody on the panel.

01:07:02.400 --> 01:07:05.130
I've heard it said that it's a
good thing that the people who

01:07:05.130 --> 01:07:07.650
avail themselves most
of government services

01:07:07.650 --> 01:07:10.350
are also the people
who vote the least.

01:07:10.350 --> 01:07:13.800
And you see that to some extent
evidenced by the 50% turnout

01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:16.920
that we see even in presidential
elections in this country.

01:07:16.920 --> 01:07:22.200
I'm wondering to what extent
you think that economic power is

01:07:22.200 --> 01:07:24.600
able to buy political
power and discourage

01:07:24.600 --> 01:07:27.990
people who might otherwise
participate from not--

01:07:27.990 --> 01:07:30.030
into not participating,
how you think

01:07:30.030 --> 01:07:33.450
that might be able to be
reversed, and what you think

01:07:33.450 --> 01:07:36.150
might happen to America
if there was 90% turnout

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:41.228
in terms of political and
economic distribution.

01:07:41.228 --> 01:07:43.770
ROBERT SOLOW: Well, I'll leave
that last part of the question

01:07:43.770 --> 01:07:47.160
to Suzanne or anybody
else, but I'm not

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:49.800
sure that it's a very good--

01:07:49.800 --> 01:07:53.580
that it's really quite
right to say that in the US,

01:07:53.580 --> 01:08:01.230
for instance, those who
use the government the most

01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:02.700
vote the least.

01:08:02.700 --> 01:08:07.440
I belong to one class of people
which directly controverts

01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:11.145
that, namely the old.

01:08:11.145 --> 01:08:13.310
KENNETH ARROW: Retired.

01:08:13.310 --> 01:08:15.690
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:15.690 --> 01:08:17.750
ROBERT SOLOW: Kenneth
wants me to say retired.

01:08:17.750 --> 01:08:21.029
That's an organizational
statement.

01:08:24.960 --> 01:08:28.080
I think that the--

01:08:28.080 --> 01:08:34.830
I'm not sure how good
that correlation would be.

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:38.399
Middle class people in
the US who are generally

01:08:38.399 --> 01:08:43.109
the ones who vote get a lot
of benefits from government.

01:08:43.109 --> 01:08:46.770
They don't much like paying
the taxes that support them,

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:50.189
but they don't mind the
benefits, and they vote.

01:08:50.189 --> 01:08:53.670
I think what governs
turnout in elections

01:08:53.670 --> 01:08:58.770
is much more
complicated than simply

01:08:58.770 --> 01:09:02.609
how much of my annual
budget comes from checks

01:09:02.609 --> 01:09:05.630
signed by the Treasury.

01:09:05.630 --> 01:09:08.343
But maybe Suzanne has
more to say about that.

01:09:08.343 --> 01:09:10.260
SUZANNE BERGER: There's
an enormous literature

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:13.229
in political science on
who votes and who does not,

01:09:13.229 --> 01:09:15.870
but what I think
is very striking

01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:18.930
is how high the abstention
rate is in the United States

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:23.537
compared to most other
advanced industrial countries.

01:09:23.537 --> 01:09:25.620
And I think there, we have
to go back to the point

01:09:25.620 --> 01:09:27.720
that Mr. Falcone
made that there are

01:09:27.720 --> 01:09:29.670
other factors that
really determine

01:09:29.670 --> 01:09:31.080
the quality of our democracy.

01:09:31.080 --> 01:09:33.840
What is the quality of the
choices that people are offered

01:09:33.840 --> 01:09:35.250
in the electoral system?

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:37.350
What are the possibilities
for participating

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:40.210
in various other-- in
various levels of government?

01:09:40.210 --> 01:09:43.319
So I think that there
is a lot at stake here

01:09:43.319 --> 01:09:45.600
and there is a puzzle
that's entered,

01:09:45.600 --> 01:09:48.812
doesn't depend uniquely
on the economic system.

01:09:48.812 --> 01:09:49.770
JOSHUA COHEN: Just on--

01:09:49.770 --> 01:09:52.680
just one-- on the
empirics of this,

01:09:52.680 --> 01:09:56.670
it is true that lower
income citizens vote

01:09:56.670 --> 01:09:59.225
at smaller rates than
higher income citizens,

01:09:59.225 --> 01:10:00.600
but people who've
actually looked

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:03.270
at the distribution
of political opinion

01:10:03.270 --> 01:10:07.980
among voters and nonvoters
conclude that there really

01:10:07.980 --> 01:10:11.700
isn't that large a difference
in the distribution

01:10:11.700 --> 01:10:13.120
of political opinion.

01:10:13.120 --> 01:10:16.290
So in order to think that
a large increase in turnout

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:18.000
would produce a
substantial difference

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:20.010
in electoral outcomes,
what you'd have to do

01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:22.110
is make a bet-- not that
this is an unsafe bet,

01:10:22.110 --> 01:10:23.610
but it's the bet
you'd have to make.

01:10:23.610 --> 01:10:26.970
You'd have to make a large bet
that the political opinions

01:10:26.970 --> 01:10:30.540
of nonvoters would be shifted
by the way they were mobilized

01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:33.130
by people who got them to vote.

01:10:33.130 --> 01:10:35.670
But if you look
statically, so to speak,

01:10:35.670 --> 01:10:37.650
the opinions of the
population as a whole

01:10:37.650 --> 01:10:43.705
are very much like the opinions
of the subset that votes.

01:10:43.705 --> 01:10:45.080
ROBERT SOLOW:
That's interesting,

01:10:45.080 --> 01:10:47.780
because that isn't the answer to
the last part of the question.

01:10:47.780 --> 01:10:49.480
JOSHUA COHEN: That's
why I said it.

01:10:49.480 --> 01:10:52.700
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:52.700 --> 01:10:55.400
Yes, go ahead.

01:10:55.400 --> 01:10:57.080
Right here in the--

01:10:57.080 --> 01:10:58.850
we need a microphone here.

01:10:58.850 --> 01:11:00.798
Oh, there's a microphone.

01:11:03.720 --> 01:11:06.290
AUDIENCE: My question
is for Professor Berger.

01:11:06.290 --> 01:11:09.830
You mentioned that about when
you were talking about rules

01:11:09.830 --> 01:11:13.040
must be decided not by
and for Formula 1 drivers,

01:11:13.040 --> 01:11:14.930
but by all people.

01:11:14.930 --> 01:11:17.240
What sort of
institutions do you think

01:11:17.240 --> 01:11:20.280
would be necessary
for that to happen?

01:11:20.280 --> 01:11:22.850
For example, Dani Rodrik
at the Kennedy School,

01:11:22.850 --> 01:11:26.570
he advocates the conflict
management institutions

01:11:26.570 --> 01:11:30.210
when she mentions democracy
and social safety nets,

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:31.760
et cetera, education levels.

01:11:31.760 --> 01:11:35.090
What, in your opinion, would
be the critical institutions

01:11:35.090 --> 01:11:40.840
necessary for full participation
and coverage as well?

01:11:40.840 --> 01:11:43.530
SUZANNE BERGER: I wish I
could give you an answer.

01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:45.720
And I think that the
hardest part of the answer

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:48.300
is that the institutions
we would need

01:11:48.300 --> 01:11:52.230
would have to be more than
national institutions.

01:11:52.230 --> 01:11:57.810
The widespread sense that the
IMF, the World Bank are somehow

01:11:57.810 --> 01:12:00.690
determining the
fortunes of societies

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:03.360
and yet citizens have
no way of effecting

01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:07.540
the decisions of the WTO or
the IMF or the World Bank,

01:12:07.540 --> 01:12:09.130
the question is, how do we--

01:12:09.130 --> 01:12:11.820
how do we provide some
measure of accountability

01:12:11.820 --> 01:12:13.560
of those institutions?

01:12:13.560 --> 01:12:15.300
Why is it that that--

01:12:15.300 --> 01:12:17.760
I mean, we do have
representatives

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:20.700
of our own national governments
sitting on those bodies,

01:12:20.700 --> 01:12:25.680
and yet it seems to most people
to be far too indirect linkage

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:28.110
to the bodies that
apparently are

01:12:28.110 --> 01:12:32.880
making direct decisions
about our social and economic

01:12:32.880 --> 01:12:33.750
welfare.

01:12:33.750 --> 01:12:35.730
So the problem that
we have to solve here

01:12:35.730 --> 01:12:39.720
is not simply how we change
our own national institutions,

01:12:39.720 --> 01:12:41.700
but how we could link--

01:12:41.700 --> 01:12:44.490
how we could link
national governments

01:12:44.490 --> 01:12:46.050
to these international bodies.

01:12:46.050 --> 01:12:49.260
That's what I think is
really the tough part here.

01:12:49.260 --> 01:12:53.260
ROBERT SOLOW: Could
I say something?

01:12:53.260 --> 01:12:58.980
I have a question about a
part of Suzanne's argument.

01:12:58.980 --> 01:13:03.600
I'm not sure it's so
clear that globalization

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:08.460
in the sense of large volume
of international trade,

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:09.510
or even perhaps--

01:13:09.510 --> 01:13:11.510
although I'm a little
more doubtful about this--

01:13:11.510 --> 01:13:14.730
a large volume of
international capital movements

01:13:14.730 --> 01:13:17.760
creates such a
drastic limitation

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:20.460
on the ability of
national governments

01:13:20.460 --> 01:13:26.400
to regulate the things
that really matter.

01:13:26.400 --> 01:13:32.340
I think the reason why
in many poor countries

01:13:32.340 --> 01:13:35.010
the national
government does not do

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:37.860
the things that the people
like us in this room

01:13:37.860 --> 01:13:41.880
would wish they would do is
not because they're exporting

01:13:41.880 --> 01:13:45.330
and importing, or even in
large part, and even often not

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:48.390
because they're dependent
on foreign capital,

01:13:48.390 --> 01:13:53.080
it's because they are
either unwise or corrupt,

01:13:53.080 --> 01:13:56.040
or in any case,
unsatisfactory governments

01:13:56.040 --> 01:13:57.540
on national grounds.

01:13:57.540 --> 01:14:01.980
There is-- globalization
has very little

01:14:01.980 --> 01:14:04.890
to do with the
tax system of even

01:14:04.890 --> 01:14:07.650
a fairly small country, which
can do more or less what

01:14:07.650 --> 01:14:09.420
it wants.

01:14:09.420 --> 01:14:12.275
The trouble is that
they don't want to do--

01:14:12.275 --> 01:14:14.400
I shouldn't put it that
way, because it's not clear

01:14:14.400 --> 01:14:17.790
whose volition is involved
here, but I don't think

01:14:17.790 --> 01:14:21.250
it's mainly globalization.

01:14:21.250 --> 01:14:26.520
Globalization provides
a nice scapegoat

01:14:26.520 --> 01:14:32.040
for a government or a party or
an institution that does not

01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:35.460
want to do something that it
might for one reason or another

01:14:35.460 --> 01:14:38.246
does not want to do.

01:14:38.246 --> 01:14:41.210
JOSHUA COHEN: I have--

01:14:41.210 --> 01:14:42.445
and then back there.

01:14:42.445 --> 01:14:43.186
Yeah, go ahead.

01:14:47.700 --> 01:14:51.855
AUDIENCE: Pursuing this
question about limitations,

01:14:51.855 --> 01:14:54.600
I'd like to ask if any
of you have reactions

01:14:54.600 --> 01:14:57.690
to the Danish decision
about 10 days ago

01:14:57.690 --> 01:15:03.330
not to join the euro
area, because at least

01:15:03.330 --> 01:15:05.460
according to the
American newspapers,

01:15:05.460 --> 01:15:11.400
the reason was that they feared
that they would have to--

01:15:11.400 --> 01:15:14.670
they couldn't maintain
their own safety net

01:15:14.670 --> 01:15:19.830
and active government to deal
with the distribution of income

01:15:19.830 --> 01:15:23.190
and power that you've
been talking about.

01:15:23.190 --> 01:15:29.250
Now Denmark is the classic
small open economy of economics,

01:15:29.250 --> 01:15:31.230
has been involved in
international trade

01:15:31.230 --> 01:15:36.690
very heavily for the
past century at least.

01:15:36.690 --> 01:15:42.840
And here, they voted against
joining a single currency area.

01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:47.760
And so I'm wondering whether
that's a critical limitation

01:15:47.760 --> 01:15:50.400
or whether this is
just the opportunity

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:55.470
to opt out of globalization or
whether something else entirely

01:15:55.470 --> 01:15:59.520
is going on in the relationship
between economic policy

01:15:59.520 --> 01:16:04.200
and the ability
of the government

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:06.600
to effect its own decisions.

01:16:09.300 --> 01:16:11.940
SUZANNE BERGER: Professor
Peter Temin, whose question

01:16:11.940 --> 01:16:15.270
concerned Denmark, is the person
from whom I've learned most

01:16:15.270 --> 01:16:18.270
about the operation
of the gold standard

01:16:18.270 --> 01:16:20.820
and the way in which
government's credibility

01:16:20.820 --> 01:16:24.390
depended in the 19th century
and parts of the 20th

01:16:24.390 --> 01:16:28.800
on government's
willingness to inflict

01:16:28.800 --> 01:16:31.930
the burden of adjustment
on its own population.

01:16:31.930 --> 01:16:34.800
And I think that that's really--

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:37.380
that in some respects,
that's the political dilemma

01:16:37.380 --> 01:16:40.450
that we're looking
at again today.

01:16:40.450 --> 01:16:44.850
That remaining in an open
market, a market which is--

01:16:44.850 --> 01:16:48.480
in a market which is open to
capital flows and short-term

01:16:48.480 --> 01:16:54.480
capital flows as well as direct
foreign direct investment,

01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:57.840
puts governments in the position
that the Asian economies were

01:16:57.840 --> 01:17:04.350
in 1997-1998 of fearing
that unless they, in fact,

01:17:04.350 --> 01:17:07.680
impose the full weight of
the adjustment on their own

01:17:07.680 --> 01:17:09.750
populations, they
would not risk--

01:17:09.750 --> 01:17:11.880
they would not be able to--

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:15.780
they would not continue
to receive capital

01:17:15.780 --> 01:17:17.050
from the outside world.

01:17:17.050 --> 01:17:20.610
I think-- I'm no expert
on Danish politics,

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:23.850
but it seems to be a
judgment of the Danes

01:17:23.850 --> 01:17:27.390
that they would be giving
up some valuable measure

01:17:27.390 --> 01:17:30.935
of political control over the
conditions of everyday life.

01:17:30.935 --> 01:17:32.310
ROBERT SOLOW:
Peter, something is

01:17:32.310 --> 01:17:34.240
rotten in the state
of Denmark here,

01:17:34.240 --> 01:17:37.590
because what I don't know--

01:17:37.590 --> 01:17:41.760
the argument that you've
described as having been given

01:17:41.760 --> 01:17:44.070
doesn't make a lot of sense.

01:17:44.070 --> 01:17:48.270
What the Danes voted against
was the European Monetary Union.

01:17:48.270 --> 01:17:51.150
It has little or nothing to do
with their ability to maintain

01:17:51.150 --> 01:17:53.040
their own social safety net.

01:17:53.040 --> 01:17:57.030
The problem with social
services and social assistance

01:17:57.030 --> 01:18:01.410
is the European Union itself,
which proposes eventually

01:18:01.410 --> 01:18:03.330
to have--

01:18:03.330 --> 01:18:07.620
to harmonize, as the
phrase goes, social policy

01:18:07.620 --> 01:18:09.960
across the members.

01:18:12.900 --> 01:18:14.610
I'm not-- I think--

01:18:14.610 --> 01:18:20.340
what-- myself-- the
Danes were on about

01:18:20.340 --> 01:18:25.980
is that they understand that
the monetary union is not

01:18:25.980 --> 01:18:30.180
an economic thing at all,
it's a political gesture.

01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:35.890
It's a gesture that amounts to
some kind of semi-commitment--

01:18:35.890 --> 01:18:39.000
commitment is much too
strong, but as a step

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:43.890
toward a political
union in Europe.

01:18:43.890 --> 01:18:47.520
And a small country
living on a little neck

01:18:47.520 --> 01:18:51.450
of land right near
Germany might not

01:18:51.450 --> 01:18:57.060
care much for a political union
in Europe, and I think myself,

01:18:57.060 --> 01:19:03.450
the vote against the EMU
was a vote against that.

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:05.700
It certainly doesn't
make sense to suppose

01:19:05.700 --> 01:19:07.800
that it would really
limit their capacity

01:19:07.800 --> 01:19:12.007
to maintain social policy.

01:19:12.007 --> 01:19:13.590
JOSHUA COHEN: All
the way in the back.

01:19:18.690 --> 01:19:23.420
AUDIENCE: This question is
addressed to Professor Berger.

01:19:23.420 --> 01:19:25.790
One of the very strong
point in your presentation,

01:19:25.790 --> 01:19:29.540
the argument was that
the national governments

01:19:29.540 --> 01:19:30.830
were doing--

01:19:30.830 --> 01:19:34.310
providing the institutional
framework in which people still

01:19:34.310 --> 01:19:36.957
within the capitalism, if
there were certain disparities

01:19:36.957 --> 01:19:39.040
or whatever, but they felt
that they had the reach

01:19:39.040 --> 01:19:41.377
and they can make
the basic choices.

01:19:41.377 --> 01:19:43.460
And the challenge is that
that's not happening now

01:19:43.460 --> 01:19:45.410
because of the globalization.

01:19:45.410 --> 01:19:48.650
And then there's a connection
in your argument saying, well,

01:19:48.650 --> 01:19:52.130
this can be seen by the fact
that the people never voted

01:19:52.130 --> 01:19:55.430
against capitalism, never
wanted to turn down the property

01:19:55.430 --> 01:19:56.450
rights.

01:19:56.450 --> 01:19:59.630
But if that's the connection,
and if I followed that

01:19:59.630 --> 01:20:02.630
correctly, to me, I
pose to you, of course,

01:20:02.630 --> 01:20:04.770
drawing from the
experiences of India,

01:20:04.770 --> 01:20:06.920
that people are not
voting against government,

01:20:06.920 --> 01:20:10.350
which are also allowing the
globalization to take place.

01:20:10.350 --> 01:20:14.090
So this argument is
that the peoples not

01:20:14.090 --> 01:20:17.420
voting against capitalist modes
of production-- capitalism

01:20:17.420 --> 01:20:19.550
and the connection of
democracy and goodness

01:20:19.550 --> 01:20:21.545
about it within the
nationalist boundary,

01:20:21.545 --> 01:20:25.633
it was evident because of people
not turning it upside-down.

01:20:25.633 --> 01:20:27.050
People are not
turning upside-down

01:20:27.050 --> 01:20:28.280
to globalization process.

01:20:28.280 --> 01:20:30.860
And their national
governments, elected by people,

01:20:30.860 --> 01:20:33.620
are taking decisions to
move into globalization

01:20:33.620 --> 01:20:35.120
and integrate it well.

01:20:35.120 --> 01:20:37.850
So, I mean, how do we see
this, too, which may appear--

01:20:37.850 --> 01:20:40.700
I mean, why would-- then there
will be a worry about this

01:20:40.700 --> 01:20:44.130
people's helplessness about
choices and not being able to--

01:20:44.130 --> 01:20:47.030
I mean, are the worried about
being overrun by the Formula 1

01:20:47.030 --> 01:20:49.310
car, where apparently,
by this logic,

01:20:49.310 --> 01:20:52.220
they are asking the
governments to do that

01:20:52.220 --> 01:20:53.846
and put them in that situation.

01:20:57.040 --> 01:21:00.520
SUZANNE BERGER: I guess we have
to ask whether people actually

01:21:00.520 --> 01:21:02.750
believe that in voting
for the governments

01:21:02.750 --> 01:21:05.710
they've chosen in
India or wherever,

01:21:05.710 --> 01:21:09.530
that they're actually
voting for globalization.

01:21:09.530 --> 01:21:13.000
In fact, parties,
elections, choices

01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:15.880
have been structured around
very different lines.

01:21:15.880 --> 01:21:19.540
And what I think is one of
the most fascinating changes

01:21:19.540 --> 01:21:21.820
over the last two
or three years is,

01:21:21.820 --> 01:21:25.960
that globalization is emerging
as an issue which people see

01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:29.440
at stake in political contests,
but it would be very difficult

01:21:29.440 --> 01:21:34.510
for me to think that
the rulers we chose

01:21:34.510 --> 01:21:38.230
or that Indians chose in
the 1980s and the 1990s,

01:21:38.230 --> 01:21:40.970
that people thought
that by their choices,

01:21:40.970 --> 01:21:43.570
they were choosing the
kind of globalized world

01:21:43.570 --> 01:21:45.460
that we have moved into.

01:21:45.460 --> 01:21:48.520
Elections weren't fought
out over those issues.

01:21:48.520 --> 01:21:50.530
What people will
think once they think

01:21:50.530 --> 01:21:52.720
about politics in
those terms, that

01:21:52.720 --> 01:21:54.670
seems to me a different point.

01:21:54.670 --> 01:21:56.960
KENNETH ARROW: That's
an observation the--

01:21:56.960 --> 01:22:00.700
it strikes me as interesting
that there are issues--

01:22:00.700 --> 01:22:02.220
and I think globalization is--

01:22:02.220 --> 01:22:07.270
I will say in the United
States and that vote from NAFTA

01:22:07.270 --> 01:22:11.590
or WTO, that are-- where the
elite, the political elite

01:22:11.590 --> 01:22:12.252
are different.

01:22:12.252 --> 01:22:13.710
Differ from the
country as a whole.

01:22:13.710 --> 01:22:16.450
Here we have two presidential
candidates firmly

01:22:16.450 --> 01:22:19.000
in favor of globalization.

01:22:19.000 --> 01:22:21.070
There's no way of voting for it.

01:22:21.070 --> 01:22:23.140
In both cases, we know
that significant members

01:22:23.140 --> 01:22:25.348
of the party-- in fact, in
the case of the Democrats,

01:22:25.348 --> 01:22:28.630
a majority of the party has
voted against the proposals

01:22:28.630 --> 01:22:32.200
which are espoused by
Vise President Gore,

01:22:32.200 --> 01:22:34.510
and certainly within
the Republican Party,

01:22:34.510 --> 01:22:36.010
while it's slightly
different there,

01:22:36.010 --> 01:22:39.910
there's a large number who would
probably follow Pat Buchanan--

01:22:39.910 --> 01:22:42.520
if they could
otherwise stand him--

01:22:42.520 --> 01:22:45.063
on this issue.

01:22:45.063 --> 01:22:46.230
And the same thing is true--

01:22:46.230 --> 01:22:48.190
the discussion about
capital punishment

01:22:48.190 --> 01:22:50.650
raises very much the
same issues in Europe.

01:22:50.650 --> 01:22:52.660
Polls suggest that
the majority of people

01:22:52.660 --> 01:22:54.270
are in favor of
capital punishment,

01:22:54.270 --> 01:22:58.010
and yet no country in Europe
has capital punishment.

01:22:58.010 --> 01:23:01.000
So there are situations in
which the political elite

01:23:01.000 --> 01:23:01.870
take stands.

01:23:01.870 --> 01:23:04.960
Now presumably if the people
felt strongly enough about it,

01:23:04.960 --> 01:23:08.620
if that became the
big issue, then--

01:23:08.620 --> 01:23:10.810
that it would overturn the
other issues and people

01:23:10.810 --> 01:23:11.560
would vote for it.

01:23:11.560 --> 01:23:14.560
But when there's obviously a
certain permissive attitude

01:23:14.560 --> 01:23:16.270
on the part of the
broad electorate,

01:23:16.270 --> 01:23:18.067
I find this issue
very interesting,

01:23:18.067 --> 01:23:19.900
and most these cases,
to be perfectly frank,

01:23:19.900 --> 01:23:22.472
I think their leader's right
and the people are wrong.

01:23:22.472 --> 01:23:25.304
[LAUGHTER]

01:23:25.304 --> 01:23:28.600
JOSHUA COHEN: Back here.

01:23:28.600 --> 01:23:31.420
AUDIENCE: I'd like to address
this question to the panel.

01:23:31.420 --> 01:23:33.080
And it's a very
general question,

01:23:33.080 --> 01:23:34.770
I hope it makes sense.

01:23:34.770 --> 01:23:36.340
And I think generally,
it has to do

01:23:36.340 --> 01:23:40.210
with the-- what might be called
the globalization of ideas,

01:23:40.210 --> 01:23:44.470
which is an old idea, and
the interaction of ideas

01:23:44.470 --> 01:23:45.310
with cultures.

01:23:45.310 --> 01:23:48.500
And I'd rather shout, if I
may, can everybody hear me?

01:23:48.500 --> 01:23:49.880
AUDIENCE: It's being recorded.

01:23:49.880 --> 01:23:50.548
There you go.

01:23:50.548 --> 01:23:51.090
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:23:53.600 --> 01:23:56.320
My question, then, is
the original question

01:23:56.320 --> 01:23:58.690
of the relationship between
democracy and capitalism.

01:23:58.690 --> 01:24:02.200
I hear on one part it's an
historical accident because it

01:24:02.200 --> 01:24:04.780
grew out of the same
media, and that you

01:24:04.780 --> 01:24:07.390
would expect certain
commonalities of values

01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:08.920
and commonalities.

01:24:08.920 --> 01:24:13.030
However, the way it evolves
has to do with the way people

01:24:13.030 --> 01:24:17.560
respond to things like the
application of brute force

01:24:17.560 --> 01:24:21.110
and the application of ideas
that spread across boundaries.

01:24:21.110 --> 01:24:23.200
And I think of this in
terms of Southeast Asia

01:24:23.200 --> 01:24:25.810
very often, because the
Southeast Asian revolts

01:24:25.810 --> 01:24:29.030
and revolutions, if you go
back to the Philippines,

01:24:29.030 --> 01:24:31.480
the Philippines became a
model for the Burmese revolt

01:24:31.480 --> 01:24:36.470
in 1988, which became a model
for the Chinese revolt in 1989,

01:24:36.470 --> 01:24:39.340
which was in a country
that was a curious mix

01:24:39.340 --> 01:24:44.590
of Chinese traditional values
and European ideas on politics.

01:24:44.590 --> 01:24:48.140
And I'm thinking that
things tend to spread,

01:24:48.140 --> 01:24:50.840
and that the apparent
correlation that

01:24:50.840 --> 01:24:54.110
remains between
capitalism and democracy

01:24:54.110 --> 01:24:55.885
is a sort of convergence.

01:24:55.885 --> 01:24:57.260
That in fact,
what's happening is

01:24:57.260 --> 01:25:00.770
you're getting a spread
of ideas in their own way,

01:25:00.770 --> 01:25:02.300
and you're also
getting people who

01:25:02.300 --> 01:25:04.217
want to develop economically,
and that there's

01:25:04.217 --> 01:25:08.801
no real necessary
correlation between the two.

01:25:08.801 --> 01:25:09.743
Thank you.

01:25:13.520 --> 01:25:16.640
ROBERT SOLOW: I wish a simple
yes or no would suffice.

01:25:16.640 --> 01:25:18.990
KENNETH ARROW: Well, let
me take a stab at it.

01:25:18.990 --> 01:25:22.375
There is-- of course,
diffusion of ideas is very old.

01:25:22.375 --> 01:25:23.750
One of the examples
I always like

01:25:23.750 --> 01:25:26.210
is the diffusion of
foods from the new world

01:25:26.210 --> 01:25:27.650
after its discovery.

01:25:27.650 --> 01:25:31.010
Think Italian cuisine
without tomatoes.

01:25:31.010 --> 01:25:33.680
The-- or Szechuan.

01:25:33.680 --> 01:25:37.110
Take something really remote
without chili peppers.

01:25:37.110 --> 01:25:39.260
These are all New World
products, so they all--

01:25:39.260 --> 01:25:42.200
all these, quote, "great
traditions" are all very recent

01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:43.550
and spread out.

01:25:43.550 --> 01:25:46.340
However, it's quite clear that
different places picked up

01:25:46.340 --> 01:25:48.390
different parts of that.

01:25:48.390 --> 01:25:51.740
To say the student revolt of
the late '60s is another example

01:25:51.740 --> 01:25:55.010
of something that gets spread
very rapidly from the United

01:25:55.010 --> 01:25:56.140
States and Europe--

01:25:56.140 --> 01:25:57.200
imitation.

01:25:57.200 --> 01:26:01.070
But it took different forms,
had a different consequence.

01:26:01.070 --> 01:26:03.320
The culture, the-- or the
needs of the particular

01:26:03.320 --> 01:26:05.570
haven't played a
big selective role,

01:26:05.570 --> 01:26:07.970
and the fact is that
some of these things

01:26:07.970 --> 01:26:12.260
fizzle out and do not remain
permanent, some catch on

01:26:12.260 --> 01:26:14.390
and there is a-- it's a
little more complicated

01:26:14.390 --> 01:26:16.880
than saying this
is simply fadism

01:26:16.880 --> 01:26:18.540
or imitation in a crude sense.

01:26:18.540 --> 01:26:19.790
There has got to be a select--

01:26:19.790 --> 01:26:21.020
it's like evolution.

01:26:21.020 --> 01:26:22.670
I mean, there's a
slight analogous to,

01:26:22.670 --> 01:26:25.350
it's not identical to it.

01:26:25.350 --> 01:26:28.650
So there's a selection--
there's a mutation element--

01:26:28.650 --> 01:26:30.950
in this case, an imitation
element which is--

01:26:30.950 --> 01:26:34.072
we don't have in biology, but
it's also a selection element,

01:26:34.072 --> 01:26:35.780
and the selection
element may be the more

01:26:35.780 --> 01:26:38.180
permanent and important
parts of the phenomena.

01:26:38.180 --> 01:26:40.910
Journalism may depend
on fads, history

01:26:40.910 --> 01:26:43.635
may depend on selection.

01:26:43.635 --> 01:26:46.010
SUZANNE BERGER: I think the
question that Julian Wheatley

01:26:46.010 --> 01:26:48.260
has posed is really an
interesting one, because I

01:26:48.260 --> 01:26:52.430
think we really do have mental
maps that link together things

01:26:52.430 --> 01:26:56.810
that might have no
necessarily relation in--

01:26:56.810 --> 01:27:01.040
necessary mechanical relation,
and that a particular kind

01:27:01.040 --> 01:27:05.150
of economic system and
a particular conceptions

01:27:05.150 --> 01:27:08.360
of political freedom have
become joined together

01:27:08.360 --> 01:27:10.952
in people's understanding
of how the world works.

01:27:10.952 --> 01:27:12.410
This is something
like what I guess

01:27:12.410 --> 01:27:15.950
Gramsci meant when he talked
about the hegemony of ideas,

01:27:15.950 --> 01:27:19.190
people's understanding that
certain ways of organizing

01:27:19.190 --> 01:27:23.150
society are necessary to a good
and decent and well-functioning

01:27:23.150 --> 01:27:24.150
society.

01:27:24.150 --> 01:27:27.050
So I think that even if we
could demonstrate that there

01:27:27.050 --> 01:27:32.050
are no necessary links between
these economic-- features

01:27:32.050 --> 01:27:34.970
of the economic system
and particular features

01:27:34.970 --> 01:27:37.910
of the political system,
that you're absolutely

01:27:37.910 --> 01:27:40.760
right, that these have now
become joined in a way where

01:27:40.760 --> 01:27:43.260
people feel that
they do, in fact,

01:27:43.260 --> 01:27:45.980
have to sign on for
the whole package,

01:27:45.980 --> 01:27:48.680
that these are not available,
that you can't sign on

01:27:48.680 --> 01:27:51.200
for one item on the
list, but it is--

01:27:51.200 --> 01:27:55.968
that they are in some way
joined as human possibility.

01:27:55.968 --> 01:27:58.010
ROBERT SOLOW: But then it
ought to be really very

01:27:58.010 --> 01:28:02.180
important to piece
out what is true

01:28:02.180 --> 01:28:04.280
and what is false
about the capacity

01:28:04.280 --> 01:28:09.020
to separate the parts of the
package-- salesman, of course,

01:28:09.020 --> 01:28:13.370
are always trying to sell
you the whole package.

01:28:13.370 --> 01:28:18.020
And I suppose part of
the wisdom of shopping

01:28:18.020 --> 01:28:20.450
is to pick out only
the part that you want.

01:28:20.450 --> 01:28:25.310
I think the problem that's
being circled around here

01:28:25.310 --> 01:28:29.360
is just that everything
that happens, so to speak,

01:28:29.360 --> 01:28:32.930
in the political,
social, economic realm

01:28:32.930 --> 01:28:35.720
is a mixture of
general principles

01:28:35.720 --> 01:28:37.730
and local peculiarities.

01:28:37.730 --> 01:28:40.700
The local peculiarities
are sometimes historical

01:28:40.700 --> 01:28:44.600
and sometimes arise
in some other way.

01:28:44.600 --> 01:28:48.380
But the same set of
principles applied

01:28:48.380 --> 01:28:50.390
in a place with one
kind of institutions

01:28:50.390 --> 01:28:53.060
will generate something
so different from what

01:28:53.060 --> 01:28:54.890
it would generate if
the institutions were

01:28:54.890 --> 01:28:58.290
a little otherwise.

01:28:58.290 --> 01:29:02.370
AUDIENCE: I've got a question
for Professor Berger.

01:29:02.370 --> 01:29:05.100
It's essentially an
objection to this argument

01:29:05.100 --> 01:29:08.730
that people don't vote
against capitalism.

01:29:08.730 --> 01:29:12.120
And in my opinion, what
happens that you can buy easily

01:29:12.120 --> 01:29:16.920
media coverage-- for
instance, the two major party

01:29:16.920 --> 01:29:20.160
participated debate and another
was not represented there.

01:29:20.160 --> 01:29:21.660
And what happened
in [INAUDIBLE] was

01:29:21.660 --> 01:29:28.320
that just two people
with liberalism ideas

01:29:28.320 --> 01:29:30.450
were facing each
other with somewhat

01:29:30.450 --> 01:29:32.640
the same global program.

01:29:32.640 --> 01:29:37.270
So, I mean, no socialist
idea were discussed there,

01:29:37.270 --> 01:29:40.760
no promise about union
rights or worker rights

01:29:40.760 --> 01:29:42.330
or stuff like that.

01:29:42.330 --> 01:29:44.640
So my opinion, it's
easy for capitalists

01:29:44.640 --> 01:29:49.170
to buy consensus among
people by agreement.

01:29:49.170 --> 01:29:51.660
It's easy to somewhat
get agreement

01:29:51.660 --> 01:29:54.925
from non-educated people
and keep them non-educated

01:29:54.925 --> 01:29:56.510
as was in the feudalist.

01:29:56.510 --> 01:30:00.720
And isn't what the same now here
in the US because good schools

01:30:00.720 --> 01:30:04.920
are not easily accessible by
poor people that can afford

01:30:04.920 --> 01:30:07.420
tuition and stuff like that.

01:30:07.420 --> 01:30:09.740
So my question is
that, it's not just

01:30:09.740 --> 01:30:12.540
capitalists buying
his own agreement,

01:30:12.540 --> 01:30:16.102
his own place in society
and in democracies?

01:30:19.880 --> 01:30:21.860
SUZANNE BERGER:
Well, I mean, I think

01:30:21.860 --> 01:30:25.375
that it is possible to
buy something in politics,

01:30:25.375 --> 01:30:26.750
and I think everyone
on the panel

01:30:26.750 --> 01:30:28.940
has acknowledged
how much in politics

01:30:28.940 --> 01:30:31.220
and how much an
electoral campaign does

01:30:31.220 --> 01:30:32.870
seem to depend on money.

01:30:32.870 --> 01:30:34.880
But if you look not
at the United States,

01:30:34.880 --> 01:30:37.520
but let's say
Europe, you'll find

01:30:37.520 --> 01:30:41.030
large anti-capitalist
social movements,

01:30:41.030 --> 01:30:44.930
large anti-capitalist political
parties, communist parties,

01:30:44.930 --> 01:30:48.080
Catholic parties that
were anti-capitalist

01:30:48.080 --> 01:30:53.100
and that did receive significant
numbers of votes in elections.

01:30:53.100 --> 01:30:56.180
I didn't say that nobody voted
for anti-capitalist parties,

01:30:56.180 --> 01:30:58.880
it's that a majority of
the electorate in none

01:30:58.880 --> 01:31:02.550
of these societies has ever
been able to get itself together

01:31:02.550 --> 01:31:06.440
on an anti-capitalist
alternative.

01:31:06.440 --> 01:31:08.750
And even in societies
in which there

01:31:08.750 --> 01:31:12.110
are strong anti-capitalists
alternatives,

01:31:12.110 --> 01:31:16.250
the majority of the electorate
has not made this choice.

01:31:16.250 --> 01:31:18.590
AUDIENCE: Actually if I--

01:31:18.590 --> 01:31:20.660
actually, three years ago in--

01:31:20.660 --> 01:31:23.990
sorry, a few years ago, that's
four years ago in Italy,

01:31:23.990 --> 01:31:27.530
the leftists wing go to
the power, so it happened.

01:31:27.530 --> 01:31:30.940
SUZANNE BERGER: Ah, but I
guess I would say that's the--

01:31:30.940 --> 01:31:36.440
it's examples like that that
sort of would prove my point.

01:31:36.440 --> 01:31:38.390
Because what had
happened to the left

01:31:38.390 --> 01:31:40.370
before they could
win this election

01:31:40.370 --> 01:31:44.010
was the left itself
had been transformed.

01:31:44.010 --> 01:31:47.000
We had the former
communist prime minister

01:31:47.000 --> 01:31:51.800
of Italy, Massimo D'Alema,
here at MIT explaining to us

01:31:51.800 --> 01:31:55.760
his profound commitment to all
of the principles of the market

01:31:55.760 --> 01:31:58.790
economy, capitalism, and
there was nothing in what

01:31:58.790 --> 01:32:01.340
he said that, in fact, any--

01:32:01.340 --> 01:32:03.203
virtually almost any
Republican might not

01:32:03.203 --> 01:32:04.370
have been able to sign onto.

01:32:04.370 --> 01:32:06.912
ROBERT SOLOW: You should keep
in mind that the United Kingdom

01:32:06.912 --> 01:32:10.240
today is governed by what is
nominally a socialist party.

01:32:25.110 --> 01:32:27.570
AUDIENCE: Could
you please comment

01:32:27.570 --> 01:32:33.480
on the tensions between
globalization and individuality

01:32:33.480 --> 01:32:35.019
and self-sufficiency?

01:32:38.400 --> 01:32:39.855
SUZANNE BERGER: Josh.

01:32:39.855 --> 01:32:43.250
[LAUGHTER]

01:32:44.845 --> 01:32:47.865
KENNETH ARROW: Well, let me
start by getting this off

01:32:47.865 --> 01:32:48.490
for both of us.

01:32:51.880 --> 01:32:54.040
Certainly when it
comes to individuality

01:32:54.040 --> 01:32:59.440
if you're meaning individuality
really on an individual basis,

01:32:59.440 --> 01:33:01.630
one could say that
human beings are never

01:33:01.630 --> 01:33:03.490
more complete than
when they're in contact

01:33:03.490 --> 01:33:04.960
with other human beings.

01:33:04.960 --> 01:33:08.440
I do not think having a
greater variety group opposed

01:33:08.440 --> 01:33:11.110
to a greater variety of
ideas, opposed to a greater

01:33:11.110 --> 01:33:13.750
variety of choices of ways--

01:33:13.750 --> 01:33:15.310
examples of ways
of life and as well

01:33:15.310 --> 01:33:19.210
as economic
alternative commodities

01:33:19.210 --> 01:33:23.850
is anything but an
increase in individuality.

01:33:23.850 --> 01:33:27.580
Now perhaps you mean
individual cultures.

01:33:27.580 --> 01:33:29.762
This is what the
people talk about.

01:33:29.762 --> 01:33:31.353
It was a French hero--

01:33:31.353 --> 01:33:32.770
true, he got three
months in jail,

01:33:32.770 --> 01:33:37.610
but still is regarded as a hero
for burning down a McDonald's.

01:33:37.610 --> 01:33:40.160
Now it's interesting-- who
goes to McDonald's in France?

01:33:40.160 --> 01:33:41.250
It's not tourists.

01:33:41.250 --> 01:33:42.910
They don't go to--

01:33:42.910 --> 01:33:47.440
it's Frenchmen who go there.

01:33:47.440 --> 01:33:50.247
And now is this--

01:33:50.247 --> 01:33:51.955
this-- it could be--
it could be regarded

01:33:51.955 --> 01:33:54.760
and is regarded, of course,
by some people as something--

01:33:54.760 --> 01:33:58.420
a deprivation of
the right of France

01:33:58.420 --> 01:34:00.600
to have its own cultural things.

01:34:00.600 --> 01:34:02.620
And these examples
usually in conflict

01:34:02.620 --> 01:34:05.050
turn out to be some kind
of local economic interest,

01:34:05.050 --> 01:34:07.670
like protecting farmers
or something like that.

01:34:07.670 --> 01:34:10.400
Well, I think you have to
ask yourself-- certainly

01:34:10.400 --> 01:34:12.740
about its self-sufficiency,
of course.

01:34:12.740 --> 01:34:15.887
International trade is a
branch of self-sufficiency.

01:34:15.887 --> 01:34:18.220
And that-- we can go into the
argument as to whether you

01:34:18.220 --> 01:34:19.690
want self-sufficiency.

01:34:19.690 --> 01:34:23.957
I think it's a bad idea
for every point of view,

01:34:23.957 --> 01:34:25.290
but that is a conceivable thing.

01:34:25.290 --> 01:34:27.880
But it's certainly--
the idea that most

01:34:27.880 --> 01:34:29.620
of this attachment
to the Japanese

01:34:29.620 --> 01:34:32.230
growing your own rice or
something like that, that's

01:34:32.230 --> 01:34:34.428
not self-sufficiency for
the individual, by the way,

01:34:34.428 --> 01:34:36.095
that self-sufficiency
to Japan, but it's

01:34:36.095 --> 01:34:39.040
no way improving
self-sufficiency

01:34:39.040 --> 01:34:40.980
of any individual.

01:34:40.980 --> 01:34:43.545
An urban Tokyo-- a
Tokyo persons is as much

01:34:43.545 --> 01:34:44.920
the victim of the
Japanese farmer

01:34:44.920 --> 01:34:48.820
as he would be of a
Thai or American farmer.

01:34:48.820 --> 01:34:51.190
Well, the-- for example,
that's an extreme case

01:34:51.190 --> 01:34:54.220
cause the Japanese actually
ordinarily exclude foreign rice

01:34:54.220 --> 01:34:56.240
completely.

01:34:56.240 --> 01:34:58.540
And one of the fears, according
to my Japanese friends,

01:34:58.540 --> 01:35:02.320
is they will find that
American rice is superior.

01:35:02.320 --> 01:35:04.586
And there's some evidence
for that, by the way.

01:35:04.586 --> 01:35:08.002
[LAUGHTER]

01:35:11.941 --> 01:35:14.530
JOSHUA COHEN: There you go.

01:35:14.530 --> 01:35:16.353
AUDIENCE: A comment
that Professor Berger

01:35:16.353 --> 01:35:18.770
can respond to, but I have a
question for Professor Solow.

01:35:18.770 --> 01:35:20.000
The comment is the race--

01:35:20.000 --> 01:35:21.560
the race car driver
analogy to me

01:35:21.560 --> 01:35:24.600
fails because there's
a kind of race where--

01:35:24.600 --> 01:35:28.345
I know one or two examples,
the drivers walk away unscathed

01:35:28.345 --> 01:35:29.720
and all the
spectators get killed

01:35:29.720 --> 01:35:31.910
and pit crew gets killed next.

01:35:31.910 --> 01:35:35.070
I know a few drivers on
beaches who are unscathed.

01:35:35.070 --> 01:35:38.150
So that's-- I don't think that
analogy works in globalization.

01:35:38.150 --> 01:35:40.730
The question I have
for Professor Solow is,

01:35:40.730 --> 01:35:42.470
of the three remedies
you mentioned,

01:35:42.470 --> 01:35:45.620
and everybody who's been
talking about buying of power,

01:35:45.620 --> 01:35:49.400
you didn't discuss the
campaign finance reform issues

01:35:49.400 --> 01:35:51.573
that have been so prominent.

01:35:51.573 --> 01:35:53.990
I'm just wondering whether
that's because it's so obvious,

01:35:53.990 --> 01:35:57.710
or specifically I'd like to ask
whether the extreme of making

01:35:57.710 --> 01:36:00.140
it a publicly-financed
system would not

01:36:00.140 --> 01:36:02.960
be a good solution,
oh whether that's not

01:36:02.960 --> 01:36:04.220
realistic to want that.

01:36:04.220 --> 01:36:08.780
ROBERT SOLOW: Well, you
guessed right, I did not--

01:36:08.780 --> 01:36:12.680
I didn't mention campaign
finance reform because it seems

01:36:12.680 --> 01:36:18.050
to me, as I suppose it seems
to everybody in this room,

01:36:18.050 --> 01:36:21.050
that it's a desperately-needed
thing and would be

01:36:21.050 --> 01:36:23.970
an improvement or
our political system,

01:36:23.970 --> 01:36:29.570
so I didn't think I needed to
make a plea for trying as best

01:36:29.570 --> 01:36:33.830
we can to diminish--

01:36:33.830 --> 01:36:37.550
probably never possible--
to exclude the role

01:36:37.550 --> 01:36:41.330
of big time money in politics.

01:36:41.330 --> 01:36:48.800
I would think-- it's a difficult
legal problem, I guess.

01:36:48.800 --> 01:36:51.290
One way-- and it would be--

01:36:51.290 --> 01:36:54.050
God knows would be better for
all of us, especially students,

01:36:54.050 --> 01:36:56.270
people who have to read and
work and things like that

01:36:56.270 --> 01:37:00.290
is to shorten the damn
political campaign

01:37:00.290 --> 01:37:03.800
and have less time for
the spending of money,

01:37:03.800 --> 01:37:05.840
but it's difficult
to see how that

01:37:05.840 --> 01:37:13.910
can be done without violating
individual rights, rights

01:37:13.910 --> 01:37:16.800
of free speech and so on.

01:37:16.800 --> 01:37:19.810
But I think that--

01:37:19.810 --> 01:37:22.160
I don't want to take
too long about this--

01:37:22.160 --> 01:37:25.220
an extremely important
advantage that we

01:37:25.220 --> 01:37:28.850
could create for ourselves
is either to go over

01:37:28.850 --> 01:37:33.230
to public financing
of political campaigns

01:37:33.230 --> 01:37:40.100
or to do some quite
drastic limitation

01:37:40.100 --> 01:37:43.150
on what can be spent.

01:37:43.150 --> 01:37:46.665
JOSHUA COHEN: Let me
go to you on the aisle.

01:37:46.665 --> 01:37:47.165
Yeah.

01:37:51.980 --> 01:37:55.130
AUDIENCE: My question
is, as everybody--

01:37:55.130 --> 01:37:57.172
you all agree that
globalization is necessarily--

01:37:57.172 --> 01:37:58.963
JOSHUA COHEN: Can you
talk into microphone?

01:37:58.963 --> 01:38:01.040
AUDIENCE: I said you all
agree that globalization

01:38:01.040 --> 01:38:02.730
is necessary.

01:38:02.730 --> 01:38:04.640
But what I'm asking
is that when America

01:38:04.640 --> 01:38:07.580
wanted to industrialize, most
countries that industrialized

01:38:07.580 --> 01:38:11.060
late after Britain have
to close their borders

01:38:11.060 --> 01:38:14.900
in order to grow the economy,
to grow the new industrial stuff

01:38:14.900 --> 01:38:16.455
to become competitive
in the market.

01:38:16.455 --> 01:38:18.830
So what I'm asking, why couldn't
the developing countries

01:38:18.830 --> 01:38:20.360
be allowed to do so?

01:38:20.360 --> 01:38:22.430
Because globalization
means opening

01:38:22.430 --> 01:38:25.910
the borders and competition
between young industries

01:38:25.910 --> 01:38:27.410
and advancing industries.

01:38:27.410 --> 01:38:30.950
So what I'm asking next is,
is globalization not a way

01:38:30.950 --> 01:38:34.430
for pushing all the polluting
industries to developing

01:38:34.430 --> 01:38:36.380
countries and making
benefit of it?

01:38:36.380 --> 01:38:38.600
If not, why can't we
allow globalization

01:38:38.600 --> 01:38:40.730
to permit movement of labor?

01:38:40.730 --> 01:38:44.000
Not only capital and investment,
but movement of labor

01:38:44.000 --> 01:38:45.860
so that it is globalization.

01:38:45.860 --> 01:38:48.320
Why is globalization
against movement of labor?

01:38:48.320 --> 01:38:50.080
Thank you.

01:38:50.080 --> 01:38:52.437
ROBERT SOLOW: I'm afraid
I didn't understand.

01:38:52.437 --> 01:38:54.020
KENNETH ARROW: One
aspect is we're not

01:38:54.020 --> 01:38:55.750
permitting immigration along
with the rest of globalization.

01:38:55.750 --> 01:38:56.850
ROBERT SOLOW: Oh, oh, oh.

01:38:56.850 --> 01:39:01.590
KENNETH ARROW: And you're right.

01:39:01.590 --> 01:39:03.520
Well, I don't who
wants to tackle this.

01:39:03.520 --> 01:39:05.940
Is this as strong as--

01:39:05.940 --> 01:39:09.920
if I understand your question
correctly, I didn't quite--

01:39:09.920 --> 01:39:13.370
you were saying
that globalization,

01:39:13.370 --> 01:39:16.580
because of free trade-- so say
industries move from the United

01:39:16.580 --> 01:39:20.990
States or other advanced
countries into the less

01:39:20.990 --> 01:39:25.320
advanced world, and then you're
worried about the-- apparently

01:39:25.320 --> 01:39:26.695
you don't regard
that as a good--

01:39:26.695 --> 01:39:29.112
I don't know why you don't
regard that as a good solution,

01:39:29.112 --> 01:39:31.640
by the way, since it enhances
the wealth of the world.

01:39:31.640 --> 01:39:34.100
You suggest that
instead of that, you're

01:39:34.100 --> 01:39:36.805
going to have
migration of labor.

01:39:36.805 --> 01:39:37.680
AUDIENCE: No, no, no.

01:39:37.680 --> 01:39:41.240
My question is, why is it that
polluting industries migrate

01:39:41.240 --> 01:39:42.240
to developing countries?

01:39:42.240 --> 01:39:44.490
Well what is happening is
the race to the bottom line,

01:39:44.490 --> 01:39:45.950
not even race to the bottom.

01:39:45.950 --> 01:39:48.270
Industries that are highly
polluting-- for example,

01:39:48.270 --> 01:39:52.100
recently America passed the
Africa Growth Opportunity Act.

01:39:52.100 --> 01:39:52.647
Under the--

01:39:52.647 --> 01:39:54.397
KENNETH ARROW: Speak
a little more slowly?

01:39:54.397 --> 01:39:55.100
I'm afraid--

01:39:55.100 --> 01:39:56.550
ROBERT SOLOW: We're not
understanding you here.

01:39:56.550 --> 01:39:57.842
AUDIENCE: Do not understand me.

01:39:57.842 --> 01:39:59.180
OK, let me start off right.

01:39:59.180 --> 01:40:04.190
Why is it that when America
wanted to industrialize,

01:40:04.190 --> 01:40:07.550
it had to close its borders
so that young industries could

01:40:07.550 --> 01:40:11.270
grow and later become effective
to compete with Britain?

01:40:11.270 --> 01:40:14.810
As France did and Germany did.

01:40:14.810 --> 01:40:17.040
Why is it that in the period
of developing countries,

01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:18.835
they would not be
allowed to do so?

01:40:18.835 --> 01:40:20.960
And why is it that the
developing countries are not

01:40:20.960 --> 01:40:24.170
allowed to take the
responsibilities of polluting

01:40:24.170 --> 01:40:25.480
industries?

01:40:25.480 --> 01:40:27.880
Because there's no the
race to the bottom line,

01:40:27.880 --> 01:40:29.210
not a race to the bottom.

01:40:29.210 --> 01:40:31.310
In this case, all the
polluting industries,

01:40:31.310 --> 01:40:33.590
the chemical industries,
textile industries

01:40:33.590 --> 01:40:35.730
are being shifted to
developing countries.

01:40:35.730 --> 01:40:39.140
And it is devastating the
environment of these areas.

01:40:39.140 --> 01:40:41.780
Now why can't we
move-- allow labor

01:40:41.780 --> 01:40:46.170
its way to move around
from developing countries

01:40:46.170 --> 01:40:47.690
to developed country?

01:40:47.690 --> 01:40:50.150
That's my question.

01:40:50.150 --> 01:40:51.770
SUZANNE BERGER: I think what--

01:40:51.770 --> 01:40:53.430
you're really asking
about two things.

01:40:53.430 --> 01:40:55.670
One, what's the relationship
between globalization

01:40:55.670 --> 01:40:59.240
and the possibilities for
growth of today's developing

01:40:59.240 --> 01:41:00.140
economies.

01:41:00.140 --> 01:41:04.170
If societies like
South Korea, Taiwan,

01:41:04.170 --> 01:41:07.400
Hong Kong were able to
grow to some extent--

01:41:07.400 --> 01:41:11.180
or Japan we're able to grow--
in part, they did do that--

01:41:11.180 --> 01:41:15.210
behind closed borders, and one
of the possibilities, if we,

01:41:15.210 --> 01:41:19.550
in fact, imposed through WTO
or imposed through the IMF

01:41:19.550 --> 01:41:23.840
and the World Bank trade
openness on today's developing

01:41:23.840 --> 01:41:26.690
economies, will we
hurt their growth?

01:41:26.690 --> 01:41:30.710
And I'll leave this question to
my economist colleagues here,

01:41:30.710 --> 01:41:33.950
but I'd like to address the
second question that you asked,

01:41:33.950 --> 01:41:36.800
and that is, why don't we
allow free migration of people

01:41:36.800 --> 01:41:38.090
across borders?

01:41:38.090 --> 01:41:41.470
And in fact, during the
first globalization,

01:41:41.470 --> 01:41:44.630
that globalization from the
middle of the 19th century

01:41:44.630 --> 01:41:47.630
until World War I,
there were far more

01:41:47.630 --> 01:41:51.290
massive migration of workers
across national borders

01:41:51.290 --> 01:41:53.690
than anything we see today.

01:41:53.690 --> 01:41:59.300
And the result of that
was that the price of--

01:41:59.300 --> 01:42:02.810
wages-- wages fell
in the societies

01:42:02.810 --> 01:42:08.720
into which the immigrants moved,
and wages rose in the societies

01:42:08.720 --> 01:42:12.410
from which the
laborers departed.

01:42:12.410 --> 01:42:17.210
And I think that there
is a keen sense in--

01:42:17.210 --> 01:42:22.370
today-- in our population
that the massive entry of new

01:42:22.370 --> 01:42:25.870
immigrants would be
bad for peoples--

01:42:25.870 --> 01:42:28.980
for the wages of the
existing population.

01:42:28.980 --> 01:42:33.040
And so this is politically
an extremely unpopular--

01:42:33.040 --> 01:42:36.860
it's an extremely
unpopular political move.

01:42:36.860 --> 01:42:37.670
That's why.

01:42:37.670 --> 01:42:40.970
Not that there is any
justice associated with it,

01:42:40.970 --> 01:42:43.730
it's the luck of the draw
which side of a border

01:42:43.730 --> 01:42:46.630
you happen to live on.

01:42:46.630 --> 01:42:49.690
ROBERT SOLOW: Let me
just add one more point,

01:42:49.690 --> 01:42:52.900
not to this particular
issue, but to what I gather

01:42:52.900 --> 01:42:54.730
now you are asking about.

01:42:58.010 --> 01:43:00.710
If one of the
characteristics or one

01:43:00.710 --> 01:43:05.540
of the accompaniments of
what's called globalization

01:43:05.540 --> 01:43:10.190
is the free movement of
capital from rich countries

01:43:10.190 --> 01:43:13.190
to poor countries,
then that is probably

01:43:13.190 --> 01:43:16.010
the single most
powerful force that

01:43:16.010 --> 01:43:19.610
is available for improving
the standard of living

01:43:19.610 --> 01:43:23.000
in poor countries.

01:43:23.000 --> 01:43:26.330
What distinguishes poor
countries from rich countries

01:43:26.330 --> 01:43:29.630
generally is that poor
countries are labor-rich

01:43:29.630 --> 01:43:33.890
and capital-poor, and rich
countries are capital-rich--

01:43:33.890 --> 01:43:37.370
not always labor-poor,
but relatively speaking,

01:43:37.370 --> 01:43:40.400
highly capital-intensive.

01:43:40.400 --> 01:43:44.960
That provides a
natural advantage both

01:43:44.960 --> 01:43:49.170
for capital-seeking profit,
which is what it tends to do,

01:43:49.170 --> 01:43:54.830
and for poor countries that find
themselves without the capital

01:43:54.830 --> 01:43:58.010
equipment that's the absolute--

01:43:58.010 --> 01:44:02.390
absolutely necessary
basis for rising incomes

01:44:02.390 --> 01:44:06.560
per person-- or rising
wages to put it bluntly.

01:44:06.560 --> 01:44:09.780
They should be welcoming
capital from abroad.

01:44:09.780 --> 01:44:15.140
There's no reason to
allow foreign corporations

01:44:15.140 --> 01:44:18.380
to dominate the politics,
to dominate the culture,

01:44:18.380 --> 01:44:23.660
to do any of those bad
things, but the capital itself

01:44:23.660 --> 01:44:26.960
ought to be welcome.

01:44:26.960 --> 01:44:28.424
JOSHUA COHEN: I had--

01:44:28.424 --> 01:44:30.376
there and then there.

01:44:30.376 --> 01:44:31.352
Yeah, go ahead.

01:44:37.210 --> 01:44:41.770
AUDIENCE: Yes, this is
directed to Professor Berger,

01:44:41.770 --> 01:44:43.090
pretty much.

01:44:43.090 --> 01:44:48.730
We live in a ever increasingly
complicated feedback

01:44:48.730 --> 01:44:50.275
network in the world.

01:44:52.960 --> 01:44:57.020
Feedback networks are very well
understood, particularly here

01:44:57.020 --> 01:45:04.000
at MIT, but they're also prone
to instability unless they're

01:45:04.000 --> 01:45:06.640
designed very, very carefully.

01:45:06.640 --> 01:45:10.092
Could you comment on that in
the context of all of this?

01:45:15.775 --> 01:45:17.233
SUZANNE BERGER:
Could you say more?

01:45:17.233 --> 01:45:20.474
[LAUGHTER]

01:45:22.800 --> 01:45:27.180
ROBERT SOLOW:
Could-- well, I'll--

01:45:27.180 --> 01:45:32.460
I think that it's
a sort of poetry

01:45:32.460 --> 01:45:36.960
to say that we live
in a feedback network

01:45:36.960 --> 01:45:39.860
and feedback networks are--

01:45:39.860 --> 01:45:41.880
have the kinds of
characteristics

01:45:41.880 --> 01:45:44.460
that you mentioned,
instabilities and whatnot,

01:45:44.460 --> 01:45:47.040
unless they're
carefully designed.

01:45:47.040 --> 01:45:50.460
But I called it
poetry because I think

01:45:50.460 --> 01:45:57.930
the direct applicability
of that sort of formulation

01:45:57.930 --> 01:46:02.190
to the kinds of problems
we are discussing here

01:46:02.190 --> 01:46:05.350
is small, very small.

01:46:05.350 --> 01:46:08.010
And the reason is
that the elements

01:46:08.010 --> 01:46:11.070
of the network that
you're talking about

01:46:11.070 --> 01:46:17.010
are human beings, corporations,
whole societies, governments,

01:46:17.010 --> 01:46:20.950
legislatures, terrorists,
God knows what.

01:46:20.950 --> 01:46:30.040
And we know very little about
the responses of those elements

01:46:30.040 --> 01:46:33.750
to simple shocks, to
simple disturbances.

01:46:33.750 --> 01:46:37.710
We don't know their operating
characteristics very well.

01:46:37.710 --> 01:46:45.000
And the notion that
you could think

01:46:45.000 --> 01:46:50.470
of a whole society, the society
we live in as a feedback

01:46:50.470 --> 01:46:55.540
network we live in, is
perhaps a good metaphor,

01:46:55.540 --> 01:46:58.690
but I don't think
at the moment--

01:46:58.690 --> 01:47:02.800
or for the next long
period of time--

01:47:02.800 --> 01:47:06.010
it's going to be a metaphor
that we can translate

01:47:06.010 --> 01:47:10.198
into diagrams and equations.

01:47:14.182 --> 01:47:15.422
JOSHUA COHEN: Yeah.

01:47:15.422 --> 01:47:16.672
AUDIENCE: OK, should I start--

01:47:16.672 --> 01:47:18.089
JOSHUA COHEN:
Microphone's coming.

01:47:24.160 --> 01:47:25.710
AUDIENCE: All right.

01:47:25.710 --> 01:47:27.870
I think Sweden has been
mentioned a couple of times

01:47:27.870 --> 01:47:31.270
here, so I might just
as well say something,

01:47:31.270 --> 01:47:35.750
because I come from Sweden and
I've been here for six weeks.

01:47:35.750 --> 01:47:40.110
And one thing that strikes--

01:47:40.110 --> 01:47:41.250
strokes-- strick?

01:47:41.250 --> 01:47:42.540
No?

01:47:42.540 --> 01:47:48.870
Strikes me as how many
more choices Americans

01:47:48.870 --> 01:47:51.360
have to make individual--

01:47:51.360 --> 01:47:58.500
individualists choices for
insurance and where to--

01:47:58.500 --> 01:48:02.760
how to invest your pensions
and what telephone company

01:48:02.760 --> 01:48:05.828
or whatever you have to--

01:48:05.828 --> 01:48:06.620
you want to choose.

01:48:06.620 --> 01:48:10.740
And this is something that
your less-regulated capitalism

01:48:10.740 --> 01:48:15.060
forces upon you, I guess, if
I could put it in that way.

01:48:15.060 --> 01:48:17.160
And I wonder, do you
think that this--

01:48:17.160 --> 01:48:23.310
all these choices in the US,
are they good for democracy?

01:48:23.310 --> 01:48:26.370
Do they make people feel
more empowered in order

01:48:26.370 --> 01:48:32.190
to participate in democratic
activity or equality things?

01:48:32.190 --> 01:48:34.860
Or is it negative
because it takes

01:48:34.860 --> 01:48:41.340
so much space of the information
flow with commercials and so--

01:48:41.340 --> 01:48:46.330
or is it irrelevant
for democracy?

01:48:46.330 --> 01:48:51.780
ROBERT SOLOW: Well,
I think it's probably

01:48:51.780 --> 01:48:56.400
accurate to say that the
availability of those choices

01:48:56.400 --> 01:49:01.540
is, in the American culture and
the American level of income,

01:49:01.540 --> 01:49:02.040
welcomed.

01:49:04.680 --> 01:49:11.080
Very few-- I have
occasionally tried

01:49:11.080 --> 01:49:14.390
the experiment of walking
into a drugstore and saying,

01:49:14.390 --> 01:49:15.490
I'd like some toothpaste.

01:49:15.490 --> 01:49:18.200
[LAUGHTER]

01:49:18.200 --> 01:49:21.420
And the salesperson says, what
kind of toothpaste do you want?

01:49:21.420 --> 01:49:24.120
And I said, I don't care, I
just want some toothpaste.

01:49:24.120 --> 01:49:26.732
And there's a--

01:49:26.732 --> 01:49:28.690
JOSHUA COHEN: And they
say, where are you from?

01:49:28.690 --> 01:49:29.130
ROBERT SOLOW: Yeah, no--

01:49:29.130 --> 01:49:30.120
[LAUGHTER]

01:49:30.120 --> 01:49:35.630
There's an-- you'd think that a
good drugstore proprietor would

01:49:35.630 --> 01:49:39.950
know what brand of toothpaste
his margin is 8/10 of 1%

01:49:39.950 --> 01:49:43.670
bigger on, and would
thrust a large tube of that

01:49:43.670 --> 01:49:44.600
into my hand.

01:49:44.600 --> 01:49:47.270
But no, no, no, I can't tell
you what toothpaste to have,

01:49:47.270 --> 01:49:50.300
you have to say what
toothpaste you want.

01:49:50.300 --> 01:49:53.440
I've even sometimes gone so
far to say, they're all alike,

01:49:53.440 --> 01:49:55.310
and-- no, no, no.

01:49:55.310 --> 01:50:00.700
But the-- it's a
funny experiment,

01:50:00.700 --> 01:50:06.710
but the point is I think valid,
this is a cultural-- cultural

01:50:06.710 --> 01:50:12.900
economic thing, but choice is
generally regarded favorably.

01:50:12.900 --> 01:50:17.000
Now we all agree, by the
way, that political choice

01:50:17.000 --> 01:50:18.420
is a good thing.

01:50:18.420 --> 01:50:21.500
One could say yes,
but you don't have

01:50:21.500 --> 01:50:26.720
to choose your portfolio
for your pension fund,

01:50:26.720 --> 01:50:28.580
and probably a lot
of people who do

01:50:28.580 --> 01:50:33.890
choose it would be better
off if somebody else chose it

01:50:33.890 --> 01:50:34.410
for them.

01:50:34.410 --> 01:50:34.910
But who?

01:50:34.910 --> 01:50:37.070
They'd have to
choose that person.

01:50:37.070 --> 01:50:42.470
In any case, I don't know how
Swedes feel about restricted

01:50:42.470 --> 01:50:47.240
choice, if they feel
themselves as being hemmed in

01:50:47.240 --> 01:50:50.360
and having choices made for
them that they would rather

01:50:50.360 --> 01:50:51.770
make for themselves.

01:50:51.770 --> 01:50:56.360
But on the whole,
it sells in the US

01:50:56.360 --> 01:51:00.170
to widen choices
that are available.

01:51:00.170 --> 01:51:03.250
KENNETH ARROW: You're
a multi-party system.

01:51:03.250 --> 01:51:06.570
So apparently when it comes to
the booths, the polling booth,

01:51:06.570 --> 01:51:09.200
you obviously have more
choice than the Americans.

01:51:09.200 --> 01:51:12.020
So-- and this doesn't seem to
disconcert anybody in Sweden,

01:51:12.020 --> 01:51:12.520
right?

01:51:16.340 --> 01:51:18.377
JOSHUA COHEN: Last question.

01:51:18.377 --> 01:51:19.960
AUDIENCE: I don't
know that I need it.

01:51:19.960 --> 01:51:22.070
I have a simple question,
and that is we've

01:51:22.070 --> 01:51:24.380
talked to everything
in a steady state,

01:51:24.380 --> 01:51:28.040
and the world population
explosion is in front of us.

01:51:28.040 --> 01:51:30.290
And it seems to me the biggest
thing that's facing us,

01:51:30.290 --> 01:51:32.780
and you haven't mentioned
what effect that will have

01:51:32.780 --> 01:51:34.880
on capitalism and democracy.

01:51:38.160 --> 01:51:42.390
ROBERT SOLOW: Well, Dr.
Baranick is certainly right

01:51:42.390 --> 01:51:48.670
that population growth is a
very, very large phenomenon

01:51:48.670 --> 01:51:54.900
and almost certainly a
problem for the world.

01:51:54.900 --> 01:52:01.980
What its effects on democracy
will be very hard to say.

01:52:01.980 --> 01:52:03.660
As the world gets
more crowded, there

01:52:03.660 --> 01:52:06.640
will be more pressure
to move across borders,

01:52:06.640 --> 01:52:11.550
and this may have influences
on the way we live

01:52:11.550 --> 01:52:15.000
or our political lives.

01:52:15.000 --> 01:52:20.220
If increasing population should
in large parts of the world--

01:52:20.220 --> 01:52:23.280
not in all--

01:52:23.280 --> 01:52:26.790
force down standards of
living, that would certainly

01:52:26.790 --> 01:52:28.960
affect both the way we
organize our economies

01:52:28.960 --> 01:52:33.785
and the way we organize
our political life.

01:52:33.785 --> 01:52:35.940
KENNETH ARROW: To pick
up on this a little bit,

01:52:35.940 --> 01:52:39.248
we notice that the
population growth

01:52:39.248 --> 01:52:41.790
is concentrated in the countries
where the democracy is least

01:52:41.790 --> 01:52:42.940
well-established.

01:52:42.940 --> 01:52:45.000
And this, I think, is the
thing-- in other words,

01:52:45.000 --> 01:52:49.350
the average degree of democracy
is going down for statistical,

01:52:49.350 --> 01:52:52.200
so to speak, just for--
on a pure counting basis.

01:52:52.200 --> 01:52:54.840
In fact, the countries
of Western Europe

01:52:54.840 --> 01:52:57.025
are showing remarkable--
and actually,

01:52:57.025 --> 01:52:59.025
almost incredible degrees
of population decline.

01:53:01.113 --> 01:53:02.530
Now of course, one
of the things--

01:53:02.530 --> 01:53:04.290
and this links back to the--

01:53:04.290 --> 01:53:06.630
links back to an
earlier observation--

01:53:06.630 --> 01:53:08.910
is that you're getting
vacuums into which

01:53:08.910 --> 01:53:10.930
immigration is induced to flow.

01:53:10.930 --> 01:53:13.250
It's true that, as
Professor Berger said,

01:53:13.250 --> 01:53:16.170
that immigration flows are
not what they were in the--

01:53:16.170 --> 01:53:17.930
around the turn of the century--

01:53:17.930 --> 01:53:20.520
the last century
ended years ago--

01:53:20.520 --> 01:53:23.640
but the-- but they are--

01:53:23.640 --> 01:53:27.640
there are large-- I mean, Italy
was a country of emigration,

01:53:27.640 --> 01:53:30.700
it's now a country
of immigration.

01:53:30.700 --> 01:53:34.920
And it changes-- there are lots
of problems in assimilation

01:53:34.920 --> 01:53:37.395
connected with
immigration, which--

01:53:37.395 --> 01:53:42.150
they are transitory, but
as your question hints,

01:53:42.150 --> 01:53:44.490
transition may be a
long period, and we've

01:53:44.490 --> 01:53:47.370
got to worry about what
happens in the transition.

01:53:47.370 --> 01:53:50.010
But the real problems
it seems to me

01:53:50.010 --> 01:53:52.200
is the growth in
countries with low--

01:53:52.200 --> 01:53:53.950
with relatively low
democratic experience.

01:53:53.950 --> 01:53:56.520
And this-- certainly if
you think of the idea,

01:53:56.520 --> 01:53:59.040
which has been suggested in
questions about international

01:53:59.040 --> 01:54:02.460
institutions and the idea of
responsibility of international

01:54:02.460 --> 01:54:06.350
institutions, it seems to me
this is an increasing block--

01:54:06.350 --> 01:54:09.570
a block to the
democratization of--

01:54:09.570 --> 01:54:12.060
and it is striking
that these institutions

01:54:12.060 --> 01:54:14.730
are what somebody once called--

01:54:14.730 --> 01:54:18.630
what [? Calabrese ?] once called
it, aresponsible agencies.

01:54:18.630 --> 01:54:20.430
They're not literally
aresponsible,

01:54:20.430 --> 01:54:23.940
but the links are they're
autonomous and they're--

01:54:23.940 --> 01:54:25.993
the European Central
Bank is incredible.

01:54:25.993 --> 01:54:27.410
There's no political
counterpoint.

01:54:27.410 --> 01:54:29.370
Here you have a central
bank running on money

01:54:29.370 --> 01:54:31.620
with no political counterpart.

01:54:31.620 --> 01:54:34.230
And in the short-run, there
are perfectly good reasons

01:54:34.230 --> 01:54:35.530
why that happens.

01:54:35.530 --> 01:54:37.760
And I'm sure it'll run
very well for a while.

01:54:37.760 --> 01:54:39.990
However, you worry
about the long-run of--

01:54:39.990 --> 01:54:42.380
what the long-run is.

01:54:42.380 --> 01:54:46.800
And I think the attempt to-- the
idea of discharging, increasing

01:54:46.800 --> 01:54:49.290
the role of international
institutions,

01:54:49.290 --> 01:54:51.770
which there are obviously
a lot of pluses,

01:54:51.770 --> 01:54:56.909
is going to be held back by
the demographic imbalance.

01:54:56.909 --> 01:54:58.326
Democratic countries
are not going

01:54:58.326 --> 01:55:00.300
to be prepared to give
up their sovereignty

01:55:00.300 --> 01:55:03.350
to such organizations.

01:55:03.350 --> 01:55:05.710
JOSHUA COHEN: It's an
instance of our theme

01:55:05.710 --> 01:55:08.830
here, that when you make
an agreement with speakers,

01:55:08.830 --> 01:55:09.580
you give--

01:55:09.580 --> 01:55:11.380
there's a kind of
contract you enter,

01:55:11.380 --> 01:55:13.640
and they get a
certain privilege,

01:55:13.640 --> 01:55:16.210
which is the privilege that you
end at the time you promised

01:55:16.210 --> 01:55:19.520
them, to end that even when
most of the people in the room

01:55:19.520 --> 01:55:22.840
will have their hands up
would like to keep going.

01:55:22.840 --> 01:55:25.214
So I'd like to
thank the speakers.

01:55:25.214 --> 01:55:28.602
[APPLAUSE]

01:55:35.378 --> 01:55:38.260
I thank all of you for
a wonderful session,

01:55:38.260 --> 01:55:43.100
and it's time to end these
two days that I thought

01:55:43.100 --> 01:55:43.880
were spectacular.

01:55:43.880 --> 01:55:45.080
Thank you very much.

01:55:45.080 --> 01:55:48.430
[APPLAUSE]

